window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-laws-of-motion","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Laws of Motion · Practice","chapter":"Laws of Motion","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A car takes an unbanked circular turn of radius 40 m. The coefficient of static friction between the tyres and the road is 0.4. What is the maximum speed at which the car can take the turn without skidding? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["12.6 m/s","4.0 m/s","16.0 m/s","20.0 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"On a level road, $v_{max} = \\sqrt{\\mu_s R g} = \\sqrt{0.4 \\times 40 \\times 10} = \\sqrt{160} \\approx 12.6$ m/s. The value 16 is what you get if you forget the square root.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A man of mass 60 kg stands on a boat of mass 120 kg that is at rest on still water. He jumps horizontally off the boat with a velocity of 3 m/s relative to the ground. Water resistance is negligible. With what speed does the boat move just after he jumps?","image":"","options":["1.5 m/s","1.0 m/s","3.0 m/s","0.9 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"There is no horizontal external force, so $60 \\times 3 = 120 \\times V$, giving $V = 1.5$ m/s opposite to the man. Dividing by the total mass (giving 0.9 m/s) is a common mistake.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A book rests on a table, and the table stands on the floor. How many of the following are action-reaction pairs?\n(a) Weight of the book and the normal force of the table on the book\n(b) Force on the table by the book and force on the book by the table\n(c) Gravitational pull of the earth on the book and gravitational pull of the book on the earth\n(d) Force by the floor on the table and the weight of the table\n(e) Force by the table on the floor and force by the floor on the table","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only (b), (c) and (e) are mutual forces between the same pair of bodies. In (a) and (d), both forces act on the same body, so they can never form an action-reaction pair, even when they are equal.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A bob of mass 0.2 kg hangs from a fixed point by a light string 0.8 m long and is set swinging in a vertical plane. At the lowest (mean) position its speed is 2 m/s. What is the tension in the string at that instant? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["3.0 N","2.0 N","1.0 N","5.0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the lowest point the net upward force provides the centripetal force: $T - mg = \\frac{mv^2}{l}$. This gives $T = 2 + \\frac{0.2 \\times 4}{0.8} = 3$ N. Taking $T = mg$ ignores the circular motion of the bob.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A particle is in equilibrium under three concurrent forces $\\vec{F}_1$, $\\vec{F}_2$ and $\\vec{F}_3$. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The resultant of $\\vec{F}_1$ and $\\vec{F}_2$ is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to $\\vec{F}_3$","The three forces can be represented by the three sides of a triangle with arrows taken in the same sense","The particle may be moving with a constant velocity","The three forces need not lie in a single plane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Since $\\vec{F}_3 = -(\\vec{F}_1 + \\vec{F}_2)$, the force $\\vec{F}_3$ lies in the plane of $\\vec{F}_1$ and $\\vec{F}_2$, so three forces in equilibrium must be coplanar. Equilibrium means zero net force, which allows either rest or uniform velocity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A car takes a circular turn on a level (unbanked) road at constant speed without skidding. Which force provides the centripetal force needed for the turn?","image":"","options":["The weight of the car","The normal reaction of the road","Static friction between the tyres and the road, acting towards the centre","Kinetic friction between the tyres and the road, acting away from the centre"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"On a level road the weight and normal reaction are vertical and cancel. Only the component of the contact force along the road surface, which is static friction, can point towards the centre. It opposes the impending outward slipping, so it is static and not kinetic friction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Impulse on a body equals the change in its momentum, even when the force and contact time cannot be found separately","In Newtonian mechanics, an impulsive force is an ordinary force that happens to be large and to act for a short time","A ball that strikes a smooth rigid wall obliquely and rebounds with the same speed exerts a force on the wall along its incoming direction of motion","A cricketer draws his hands back while catching so that the same change in momentum occurs over a longer time, needing a smaller force"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For an oblique rebound with unchanged speed, only the momentum component normal to the wall reverses; the component parallel to the wall stays the same. So the impulse, and hence the force on the wall, is normal to the wall, not along the incoming direction. The other three statements agree with NCERT.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"When the branch of a fruit tree is shaken vigorously, ripe fruits get detached and fall. This is best explained by:","image":"","options":["Inertia of the fruits, which tend to stay in their original state while the branch moves","The third law reaction of the branch pushing the fruits away","An increase in the gravitational force on the fruits during shaking","The centripetal force acting outward on the fruits"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The branch is suddenly set into motion, but the fruits resist this change of state because of inertia. The weak stalk then breaks. Gravity does not increase, and centripetal force is never outward.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform rope of mass 2 kg hangs vertically and carries a 3 kg block at its lower end. The rope is pulled up from its top end so that the system accelerates upward at 2 m/s$^2$. What is the tension at the midpoint of the rope? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["48 N","60 N","40 N","36 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Below the midpoint hang the block and half the rope, a total of 4 kg. For this part, $T - 4g = 4a$, so $T = 4 \\times 12 = 48$ N. 60 N is the tension at the top, which supports the whole 5 kg.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"An observer sits inside a car that moves on a straight road with acceleration $\\vec{a}$. To apply Newton's second law in the car's own frame, the observer must add a pseudo force on every body of mass m. This pseudo force is","image":"","options":["$m\\vec{a}$, in the direction of the car's acceleration","$m\\vec{a}$, opposite to the car's acceleration","$mg$, vertically downward","zero, because the car is a material frame"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In an accelerating frame, a pseudo force $-m\\vec{a}$ (opposite to the frame's acceleration) must be added. This is why a passenger seems pushed backward when the car speeds up. It is not a force exerted by any body, so it has no third-law partner.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A man of mass 60 kg stands on a cart of mass 40 kg that is at rest on frictionless ice. He jumps off horizontally with a velocity of 5 m/s relative to the cart. With what speed does the cart move relative to the ground just after the jump?","image":"","options":["3 m/s","7.5 m/s","2 m/s","5 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the cart recoil with speed V. The man's ground velocity is then $5 - V$, and momentum conservation gives $60(5 - V) = 40V$, so $V = 3$ m/s. Treating 5 m/s as the man's ground velocity gives the trap answer of 7.5 m/s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (force that acts as the centripetal force).\nList-I\n(A) Stone whirled in a horizontal circle by a string\n(B) Planet orbiting the sun\n(C) Car turning on a level road\n(D) Car turning on a frictionless banked road\nList-II\n(I) Static friction\n(II) Horizontal component of the normal reaction\n(III) Tension\n(IV) Gravitational force due to the sun","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Centripetal force is not a separate kind of force; it is always some real force. Here those forces are tension for the stone, gravity for the planet, static friction on a level road, and the inward horizontal component of $N$ on a frictionless banked road.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Impulse and momentum have the same SI unit, since 1 N s = 1 kg m/s.\n(b) The impulse on a body always points along its final momentum.\n(c) In Newtonian mechanics, an impulsive force obeys the same laws as any other force; it is simply large and acts for a short time.\n(d) A ball strikes a smooth rigid wall obliquely and rebounds with unchanged speed. The impulse on the ball by the wall is perpendicular to the wall.\n(e) Equal impulses given to a light body and a heavy body, both initially at rest, give them equal speeds.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (c) and (d) are correct. Impulse is along the change in momentum, not along the final momentum, so (b) is wrong. Equal impulses give equal momenta, so the lighter body moves faster, which makes (e) wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A person of mass 60 kg stands on a weighing scale in a lift (g = 10 m/s$^2$). Match the state of the lift in List-I with the scale reading in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Accelerating upward at 3 m/s$^2$\n(B) Accelerating downward at 3 m/s$^2$\n(C) Moving upward with constant velocity\n(D) Falling freely\nList-II\n(I) 420 N\n(II) 0 N\n(III) 780 N\n(IV) 600 N","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reading is $R = m(g+a)$ with a taken positive upward. This gives 60×13 = 780 N, 60×7 = 420 N, 600 N at constant velocity, and 0 N in free fall. The trap is linking 'moving upward' with a larger reading even when the acceleration is zero.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Newton's third law is correct?","image":"","options":["The action force acts first, and the reaction follows a short time later","Action is the cause and reaction is its effect","The force on A by B and the force on B by A act at the same instant, and either one may be called the action","The third law applies only to bodies in contact with each other"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The third law implies no time order and no cause-effect relation; the two mutual forces are simultaneous and the labels are interchangeable. The law also holds for non-contact forces such as gravity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two billiard balls each of mass 0.05 kg moving in opposite directions with speed 6 m s$^{-1}$ collide and rebound with the same speed. What is the magnitude of the impulse imparted to each ball due to the other?","image":"","options":["0.6 kg m s$^{-1}$","0.3 kg m s$^{-1}$","zero","1.2 kg m s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each ball's velocity reverses from +6 to -6 m s$^{-1}$. The impulse is therefore 0.05 × 12 = 0.6 kg m s$^{-1}$, with equal and opposite impulses on the two balls.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.18, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body with mass 5 kg is acted upon by a force $\\vec{F} = (-3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ N. If its initial velocity at t = 0 is $\\vec{v} = (6\\hat{i} - 12\\hat{j})$ m s$^{-1}$, the time at which it will just have a velocity along the y-axis is","image":"","options":["never","10 s","2 s","15 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The x-acceleration is $-3/5 = -0.6$ m s$^{-2}$, so $v_x = 6 - 0.6t$. Setting $v_x = 0$ gives t = 10 s, when the velocity is purely along y.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a person pushes two blocks P and Q in contact across a smooth floor so that both accelerate, the force exerted by P on Q has the same magnitude as the force exerted by Q on P.\nReason (R): The mutual forces between two bodies are equal and opposite at every instant, whatever the state of motion of the bodies.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the third law, $F_{PQ} = -F_{QP}$ holds whether the bodies are at rest or accelerating. A frequent misconception is that the 'pushing' block exerts the larger force. In fact, the system accelerates only because of the external push.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A hockey player is moving northward and suddenly turns westward with the same speed to avoid an opponent. The force that acts on the player is","image":"","options":["frictional force along westward.","muscle force along southward.","frictional force along south-west.","muscle force along south-west."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The change in velocity is (west) - (north), which points south-west, so the force must also point south-west. This external force on the player is supplied by friction from the ground.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The driver of a three-wheeler moving with a speed of 36 km/h sees a child standing in the middle of the road and brings his vehicle to rest in 4.0 s just in time to save the child. What is the average retarding force on the vehicle? The mass of the three-wheeler is 400 kg and the mass of the driver is 65 kg.","image":"","options":["1000 N","1162.5 N","162.5 N","4185 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"36 km/h is 10 m s$^{-1}$, so the retardation is 10/4 = 2.5 m s$^{-2}$. The total mass is 465 kg, so F = 465 × 2.5 ≈ 1.16 × 10$^3$ N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.8, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A road is banked at angle $\\theta$, and the coefficient of static friction between tyres and road is $\\mu_s$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) At the optimum speed $v_o = \\sqrt{Rg\\tan\\theta}$, the friction force on the car is zero.\n(b) If the car moves slower than $v_o$, friction on the car acts up the slope.\n(c) A car can remain parked on this banked road only if $\\tan\\theta \\le \\mu_s$.\n(d) The maximum safe speed on the banked road is larger for a heavier car.\n(e) When the car moves at $v_o$, the normal reaction on it equals its weight $mg$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (c) agree with NCERT, so three are correct. Statement (d) is wrong because mass cancels in the expression for $v_{max}$. Statement (e) is wrong because at $v_o$ the vertical balance $N\\cos\\theta = mg$ gives $N = \\frac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}$, which is greater than $mg$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A gun and a bullet are initially at rest. The gun is then fired.\nStatement I: Just after firing, the total momentum of the (gun + bullet) system is zero.\nStatement II: The force on the bullet by the gun and the force on the gun by the bullet are equal and opposite, and they act for the same time interval.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the third law the mutual forces are equal and opposite, and they act over a common interval $\\Delta t$. Their impulses therefore cancel. Since the system started at rest, the total momentum stays zero, so both statements are true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A small block is placed on the smooth inclined face of a wedge of angle 37° with the horizontal. The wedge is pushed along a horizontal floor so that its inclined face presses against the block. With what acceleration must the wedge move so that the block stays at rest relative to the wedge? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$, $\\tan 37° = \\frac{3}{4}$)","image":"","options":["7.5 m/s$^2$","6.0 m/s$^2$","8.0 m/s$^2$","13.3 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only gravity and the normal force act on the block. Vertically, $N\\cos\\theta = mg$; horizontally, $N\\sin\\theta = ma$. So $a = g\\tan\\theta = 7.5$ m/s$^2$. Using $g\\sin\\theta$ or $g\\cos\\theta$ gives the wrong values 6 or 8 m/s$^2$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Static friction\n(B) Kinetic friction\n(C) Rolling friction\n(D) Limiting friction\nList-II\n(P) Adjusts its magnitude to oppose impending motion\n(Q) Equals $\\mu_k N$ and is nearly independent of velocity\n(R) Arises from momentary deformation giving a finite contact area\n(S) Equals $\\mu_s N$, the maximum value of static friction","image":"","options":["A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P","A-P, B-R, C-Q, D-S","A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Static friction is self-adjusting and opposes impending motion. Kinetic friction equals $\\mu_k N$ and is nearly velocity-independent. Rolling friction comes from slight deformation of the surfaces in contact. Limiting friction is the ceiling value $\\mu_s N$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a car on a circular road banked at angle $\\theta$, with coefficient of static friction $\\mu_s$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) If the car moves slower than the optimum speed $v_o$, the frictional force on it acts up the slope.\n(ii) The car can remain parked on the banked road only if $\\tan\\theta \\le \\mu_s$.\n(iii) For the same $\\mu_s$ and radius, the maximum safe speed on the banked road is greater than on a level road.\n(iv) At the optimum speed, static friction takes its maximum value $\\mu_s N$.\n(v) The optimum speed $v_o$ is larger for a heavier car.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii) and (iii) agree with NCERT's analysis of the banked road. At the optimum speed friction is zero, not maximum, so (iv) is wrong. Since $v_o = \\sqrt{Rg\\tan\\theta}$ does not depend on mass, (v) is also wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block rests on a rough plane inclined at $30^\\circ$ to the horizontal. The coefficient of static friction is $\\mu_s = 0.7$. What is the magnitude of the frictional force on the block? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["12.1 N","17.3 N","10 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Since $\\tan 30^\\circ \\approx 0.58 < 0.7$, the block remains at rest. Static friction then just balances the component of gravity along the slope: $mg\\sin 30^\\circ = 10$ N. The value 12.1 N is $\\mu_s N$, the limiting friction, which is not reached here.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A box that stays at rest relative to the floor of an accelerating train is acted on by static friction directed along the train's acceleration.\nStatement II: For a given pair of surfaces, the coefficient of kinetic friction is greater than the coefficient of static friction.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Friction is the only horizontal force on the box, so it must point along the train's acceleration in order to accelerate the box with the train. Experiments show that $\\mu_k < \\mu_s$, so Statement II is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Match each force in List-I with its third-law partner in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Force on the bullet by the gun\n(B) Gravitational force on the Moon by the Earth\n(C) Forward friction on a walking person's foot by the ground\n(D) Upward force on a hanging bob by the string\nList-II\n(i) Gravitational force on the Earth by the Moon\n(ii) Downward force on the string by the bob\n(iii) Force on the gun by the bullet\n(iv) Backward friction on the ground by the foot","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each partner force is obtained by swapping 'on' and 'by' and reversing the direction. Note that the earth's gravity on the bob is not the partner of the string's pull on the bob.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 5 kg is acted upon by two perpendicular forces 8 N and 6 N. What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the body?","image":"","options":["2.8 m s$^{-2}$","0.4 m s$^{-2}$","10 m s$^{-2}$","2.0 m s$^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The resultant force is $\\sqrt{8^2 + 6^2} = 10$ N, so a = 10/5 = 2.0 m s$^{-2}$. It acts at $\\tan^{-1}(3/4) \\approx 37°$ to the 8 N force; simply adding the forces (14 N) is the common mistake.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.7, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a book lying at rest on a horizontal table, the normal force by the table on the book is equal in magnitude to the weight of the book.\nReason (R): The weight of the book and the normal force on it by the table form an action-reaction pair.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The normal force equals the weight because the book is observed at rest, so by the first law the net force on it is zero. Both forces act on the same body, so they cannot be an action-reaction pair. The reaction to the earth's pull on the book is the book's pull on the earth.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is thrown vertically upward. Neglect air resistance. At the highest point of its path, which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["Velocity is zero, so the net force and acceleration are also zero","Velocity is zero, but the acceleration is g downward and the net force is mg downward","Acceleration is zero, but the net force is mg downward","The net force on the ball reverses from downward to upward at that point"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A body momentarily at rest need not have zero force or acceleration. At the top, the only force is still the weight mg, so the acceleration is g downward. Assuming zero velocity means zero force is the common trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A uniform heavy rope hanging vertically from a ceiling has the same tension at every point along its length.\nStatement II: Taking the tension to be the same throughout a string is valid only when the mass of the string is negligible.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At any point, the tension in a heavy hanging rope must support the weight of the rope below that point. It is therefore largest at the top and zero at the free end, so Statement I is wrong. NCERT notes that constant tension holds only for a string of negligible mass, so Statement II is correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg block rests on a smooth incline of angle $37^\\circ$. It is held stationary by a horizontal force $F$ pushing it towards the incline. What is the normal force exerted by the incline on the block? Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$, $\\sin 37^\\circ = 0.6$ and $\\cos 37^\\circ = 0.8$.","image":"","options":["62.5 N","40 N","50 N","37.5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"There is no friction, so vertical balance alone gives $N\\cos 37^\\circ = mg$, and $N = 50/0.8 = 62.5$ N; the required push is $F = mg\\tan 37^\\circ = 37.5$ N. Writing $N = mg\\cos\\theta$ (40 N) is wrong here, because the horizontal force also has a component pressing the block into the surface.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.2 kg moving horizontally at 15 m/s strikes a vertical wall normally. It rebounds along the same line with a speed of 10 m/s. What is the magnitude of the impulse imparted to the ball by the wall?","image":"","options":["1 N s","5 N s","3 N s","2.5 N s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The velocity reverses direction, so $\\Delta p = m(v_f - v_i) = 0.2\\,[10-(-15)] = 5$ N s. Subtracting the speeds directly gives the trap value of 1 N s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A car of mass 1000 kg moves at a constant speed of 10 m/s on a circular road of radius 30 m. The road is banked at angle $\\theta$, where $\\sin\\theta = 0.6$ and $\\cos\\theta = 0.8$. The car does not slip. What is the magnitude of the static frictional force acting on the car? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["3.33 kN","2.67 kN","6.00 kN","Zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The optimum speed is $v_o = \\sqrt{Rg\\tan\\theta} = 15$ m/s. Since 10 m/s is less than this, friction acts up the slope. Along the incline, $mg\\sin\\theta - f = m\\frac{v^2}{R}\\cos\\theta$, so $f = 1000(6 - 2.67) \\approx 3.33$ kN. Friction is zero only at exactly $v_o$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about friction are correct?\n(a) The limiting value of static friction does not depend on the area of contact.\n(b) The coefficient of kinetic friction is generally less than the coefficient of static friction.\n(c) Kinetic friction is nearly independent of the relative speed of the surfaces.\n(d) A block resting on a horizontal table experiences static friction even when no horizontal force acts on it.\n(e) The laws of friction are fundamental laws, like the law of gravitation.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (c) agree with NCERT. Statement (d) is wrong because static friction comes into play only when there is an applied force or impending motion. Statement (e) is wrong because the laws of friction are approximate empirical relations, not fundamental laws.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 10 kg block is suspended at rest by two light strings tied to a horizontal ceiling. One string makes $30^\\circ$ with the vertical and the other makes $60^\\circ$ with the vertical. What is the tension in the string inclined at $60^\\circ$ to the vertical? Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$.","image":"","options":["50 N","86.6 N","100 N","57.7 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Horizontal balance gives $T_1 \\sin 30^\\circ = T_2 \\sin 60^\\circ$, so $T_1 = \\sqrt{3}T_2$. Vertical balance then gives $\\frac{3}{2}T_2 + \\frac{1}{2}T_2 = 100$, so $T_2 = 50$ N. The string closer to the vertical carries the larger tension, 86.6 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A knot is held in equilibrium by three strings with tensions $T_1$, $T_2$ and $T_3$. The angle between $T_1$ and $T_2$ is $150^\\circ$, between $T_2$ and $T_3$ is $90^\\circ$, and between $T_3$ and $T_1$ is $120^\\circ$. If $T_1 = 40$ N, what is $T_3$?","image":"","options":["20 N","34.6 N","40 N","80 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For three concurrent forces in equilibrium, each force is proportional to the sine of the angle between the other two (Lami's theorem). So $T_3/\\sin 150^\\circ = T_1/\\sin 90^\\circ$, giving $T_3 = 40 \\times 0.5 = 20$ N. Using the angle adjacent to $T_3$ instead of the opposite one is the common error.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a bat hits a ball, the impulse on the ball can be determined even though the force exerted by the bat and the time of contact are not separately known.\nReason (R): Impulse equals the change in momentum of the ball, which can be found from its velocities just before and just after the hit.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Impulse is the product of force and time, and it equals the change in momentum. Because the momentum change is measurable from the initial and final velocities, the impulse is known even when the force and the contact time are not. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A smooth inclined plane of angle 30° is fixed to the floor of a lift. The lift accelerates upward at 2 m/s$^2$. A block is released from rest on the incline. What is the acceleration of the block relative to the incline? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["5 m/s$^2$","4 m/s$^2$","6 m/s$^2$","7 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In the lift frame, a pseudo force ma acts downward and adds to gravity, giving an effective g of $g + a = 12$ m/s$^2$. The acceleration down the smooth incline is then $12\\sin 30° = 6$ m/s$^2$. The common slip is to use g alone, which gives 5 m/s$^2$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Action and reaction forces, though equal and opposite, never cancel each other when we study the motion of a single body.\nReason (R): Action and reaction forces act on two different bodies.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For any one body, only one force of the pair acts on it, so adding the pair to get a net force of zero is an error. The reason, that the two forces act on different bodies, directly explains this.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Blocks A and B are joined by a light string and lie on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal force F pulls block B, and the string drags block A along. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The tension in the connecting string does not appear in the free-body diagram of the combined system (A + B)","The acceleration of the system is $\\frac{F}{m_A + m_B}$","In the free-body diagram of block B alone, the string tension appears as an external force acting backward on B","The net force on the combined system is zero, because the tension forces on A and B cancel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The tension forces on A and B are internal to the system and do cancel. However, the external force F remains, so the net force on the system is F, not zero. Internal forces cancel in pairs, but that does not make the net external force zero.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.4 kg moving at 10 m/s is brought to rest by a fielder in 0.05 s. What is the magnitude of the average force exerted by the fielder on the ball?","image":"","options":["80 N","8 N","200 N","20 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The average force is $\\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{0.4 \\times 10}{0.05} = 80$ N. Writing 8 N comes from multiplying by the time instead of dividing by it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"In an ideal Atwood machine (light string, massless frictionless pulley), masses $m_1 > m_2$ are released from rest. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The tension lies between $m_2 g$ and $m_1 g$.\n(ii) The magnitude of the acceleration is less than g.\n(iii) The force exerted by the support on the pulley is 2T, which is less than $(m_1 + m_2)g$.\n(iv) If the masses were equal and given a small push, they would move with constant velocity.\n(v) The tension equals $\\frac{(m_1 + m_2)g}{2}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The tension is $T = \\frac{2m_1 m_2 g}{m_1+m_2}$, which lies between the two weights, and $a = \\frac{(m_1-m_2)g}{m_1+m_2} < g$. Since $2T = \\frac{4m_1m_2g}{m_1+m_2} < (m_1+m_2)g$, statement (iii) is true, and equal masses give zero net force, so (iv) is true. Statement (v) is false, because T is less than the arithmetic mean of the weights.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.5 kg moving at 20 m/s is brought to rest by a constant force in 0.1 s. What is the magnitude of this force?","image":"","options":["10 N","50 N","100 N","200 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The force equals the rate of change of momentum: $F = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{0.5 \\times 20}{0.1} = 100$ N. The common slip is to forget to divide by the time interval.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The frictional force on a body always acts opposite to the direction of motion of that body.\nStatement II: For a box lying at rest relative to the floor of a train that is accelerating forward, the static friction on the box acts in the forward direction.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Friction opposes relative motion between surfaces in contact, not the motion of the body itself. For the box, the impending relative motion is backward, so static friction acts forward. This forward friction is the only horizontal force accelerating the box along with the train, which makes Statement II correct and Statement I incorrect.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Sand falls vertically onto a horizontal conveyor belt at a steady rate of 2 kg/s. The belt is kept moving at a constant speed of 5 m/s. Neglecting friction in the belt mechanism, what horizontal force must be applied to the belt to keep its speed constant?","image":"","options":["10 N","0 N, since the belt does not accelerate","5 N","20 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each second, 2 kg of sand that had no horizontal velocity is brought to 5 m/s. The horizontal momentum of the system therefore grows at $v\\frac{dm}{dt} = 5 \\times 2 = 10$ kg m/s^2, and by $F = \\frac{dp}{dt}$ the force is 10 N. Zero is the trap: constant velocity does not mean zero force when the mass being moved keeps increasing.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform rope of mass 4 kg and length 2 m lies on a smooth horizontal surface. It is pulled at one end by a horizontal force of 20 N. What is the tension in the rope at a point 0.5 m from the end being pulled?","image":"","options":["15 N","5 N","20 N","10 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The whole rope accelerates at $a = 20/4 = 5$ m/s$^2$. The tension at the chosen point must accelerate the 1.5 m of rope behind it, which has a mass of 3 kg, so $T = 3\\times 5 = 15$ N. Using the 0.5 m segment in front of the point instead gives the wrong value of 5 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Conservation of momentum in a collision between particles can be understood from","image":"","options":["conservation of energy.","Newton's first law only.","Newton's second law only.","both Newton's second and third law."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"By the third law, the colliding particles exert equal and opposite forces on each other for the same time. By the second law, these forces produce equal and opposite changes in momentum, so the total momentum stays constant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Block A (2 kg) lies on a rough horizontal table, with $\\mu_k = 0.25$ between A and the table. A light string attached to A passes over a smooth pulley at the table's edge and supports a hanging block B (3 kg). A 1 kg block C sits on top of A and moves with A without slipping. When the system is released, what is the magnitude of the friction force exerted on C by A? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["3.75 N","2.5 N","10 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The table's normal force on A is 30 N (A and C together), so kinetic friction is 7.5 N. For the whole system, $a = \\frac{30 - 7.5}{6} = 3.75$ m/s$^2$. Only static friction accelerates C, so it equals $1 \\times 3.75 = 3.75$ N. Choosing 0 N wrongly assumes no friction acts on a block that is not slipping.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A book rests on a horizontal table. Which of the following is an action-reaction pair according to Newton's third law?","image":"","options":["Weight of the book and the normal force exerted by the table on the book","Force exerted by the book on the table and force exerted by the table on the book","Weight of the book and the force exerted by the book on the table","Normal force on the book by the table and the weight of the table"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An action-reaction pair consists of mutual forces between the same two bodies, acting on different bodies. The weight and the normal force both act on the book, so they are not a pair. They are equal only because the book is in equilibrium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg mass hangs from a ceiling by a light string. A horizontal force F is applied to the mass, so that in equilibrium the string makes an angle of $37^\\circ$ with the vertical. What is the tension in the string? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$, $\\sin 37^\\circ = 0.6$, $\\cos 37^\\circ = 0.8$.)","image":"","options":["62.5 N","50 N","37.5 N","83.3 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Balancing the vertical components gives $T\\cos 37^\\circ = mg$, so $T = 50/0.8 = 62.5$ N. The value 37.5 N is the horizontal force F ($mg\\tan 37^\\circ$), not the tension. The tension must exceed the weight, because it also balances F.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Block A of mass 2 kg rests on block B of mass 4 kg. Block B lies on a smooth horizontal floor, and the coefficient of static friction between A and B is 0.3. A horizontal force F is applied to the lower block B. What is the largest value of F for which the two blocks move together without slipping? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["6 N","12 N","18 N","24 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Only static friction accelerates A, so its maximum acceleration is $\\mu_s g = 3$ m/s$^2$. Both blocks must share this acceleration, so $F_{max} = (2+4) \\times 3 = 18$ N. The 6 N option is just the limiting friction on A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Blocks A (2 kg) and B (3 kg) are joined by a light string on a rough horizontal floor. The coefficient of kinetic friction is 0.2 for both blocks. A horizontal force of 30 N pulls block B, and both blocks slide with the string taut. (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)\nStatement I: The tension in the string is 12 N.\nStatement II: If the coefficient of kinetic friction for both blocks were 0.4 (with the blocks still sliding), the tension in the string would remain unchanged.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"With $\\mu = 0.2$: $a = \\frac{30 - 10}{5} = 4$ m/s$^2$ and $T = 2 \\times 4 + 0.2 \\times 20 = 12$ N. With $\\mu = 0.4$: $a = 2$ m/s$^2$ and $T = 4 + 8 = 12$ N again. In general $T = \\frac{F m_A}{m_A + m_B}$ when both blocks have the same coefficient, so friction drops out.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two blocks of masses 2 kg and 3 kg are placed in contact on a smooth horizontal floor. A horizontal force of 20 N pushes the 2 kg block, so that it pushes the 3 kg block ahead of it. What is the magnitude of the contact force between the two blocks?","image":"","options":["12 N","8 N","20 N","4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The common acceleration is $\\frac{20}{5} = 4$ m/s$^2$. The only horizontal force on the 3 kg block is the contact force, so it equals $3 \\times 4 = 12$ N. A common trap is to take 8 N, which is the mass of the pushed block (2 kg) times the acceleration.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Block A (mass 2 kg) is placed on top of block B (mass 3 kg). Block B rests on a frictionless horizontal floor. Between A and B, the coefficient of static friction is 0.4 and the coefficient of kinetic friction is 0.3. A horizontal force of 20 N is applied to block A. What is the magnitude of the acceleration of A relative to B? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["5 m/s$^2$","4 m/s$^2$","7 m/s$^2$","2 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If the blocks moved together, $a = 20/5 = 4$ m/s$^2$, and B would need 12 N of friction. The maximum static friction is only $0.4\\times 20 = 8$ N, so A slips on B. Kinetic friction of $0.3\\times 20 = 6$ N then gives $a_A = (20-6)/2 = 7$ m/s$^2$ and $a_B = 6/3 = 2$ m/s$^2$, so the relative acceleration is 5 m/s$^2$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two masses of 3 kg and 5 kg hang at the ends of a light inextensible string passing over a frictionless pulley. The system is released from rest. What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the masses? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["2.5 m/s$^2$","5 m/s$^2$","1.25 m/s$^2$","10 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The net driving force is $(5-3)g = 20$ N. This force acts on the total mass of 8 kg, so $a = \\frac{20}{8} = 2.5$ m/s$^2$. Dividing by only one of the masses gives a wrong answer.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A cricket ball of mass 150 g has an initial velocity $\\vec{u} = (3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m s$^{-1}$ and a final velocity $\\vec{v} = -(3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m s$^{-1}$ after being hit. The magnitude of the momentum transferred during the hit is","image":"","options":["Zero","0.75 kg m s$^{-1}$","1.5 kg m s$^{-1}$","14 kg m s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The change in momentum is $-(0.9\\hat{i} + 1.2\\hat{j})$ kg m s$^{-1}$. Its magnitude is $\\sqrt{0.9^2 + 1.2^2} = 1.5$ kg m s$^{-1}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.4, made self-contained)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A constant retarding force of 50 N is applied to a body of mass 20 kg moving initially with a speed of 15 m s$^{-1}$. How long does the body take to stop?","image":"","options":["2.5 s","6 s","0.4 s","12 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The retardation is 50/20 = 2.5 m s$^{-2}$. Using v = u - at with v = 0 gives t = 15/2.5 = 6 s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.5, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stone of mass 0.25 kg tied to the end of a string is whirled round in a circle of radius 1.5 m in a horizontal plane. What is the maximum speed with which the stone can be whirled around if the string can withstand a maximum tension of 200 N?","image":"","options":["28 m s$^{-1}$","20 m s$^{-1}$","49 m s$^{-1}$","34.6 m s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"At the limit, $T_{max} = mv^2/r$. So $v = \\sqrt{200 \\times 1.5/0.25} = \\sqrt{1200} \\approx 35$ m s$^{-1}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.21 second part, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Three blocks of masses 1 kg, 2 kg and 3 kg lie in a line on a smooth horizontal surface, connected by light inextensible strings in that order. A horizontal force of 12 N pulls the 3 kg block away from the others. What is the tension in the string connecting the 2 kg and 3 kg blocks?","image":"","options":["2 N","6 N","10 N","12 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The common acceleration is 12/6 = 2 m/s^2. The string between the 2 kg and 3 kg blocks must accelerate the 1 kg and 2 kg blocks behind it, so T = 3 × 2 = 6 N. A common trap is using only the 2 kg mass (giving 4 N), or thinking the full 12 N is transmitted.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Maximum static friction on a block of mass m resting on a horizontal surface\n(B) Angle of an incline at which a block just begins to slide\n(C) Retardation of a block sliding on a rough horizontal floor after the applied force is removed\n(D) Maximum speed of a car on an unbanked circular turn of radius R\nList-II\n(P) $\\tan^{-1}\\mu_s$\n(Q) $\\mu_s mg$\n(R) $\\sqrt{\\mu_s R g}$\n(S) $\\mu_k g$","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R","A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S","A-S, B-P, C-Q, D-R","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Limiting friction is $\\mu_s N = \\mu_s mg$, and the angle of repose satisfies $\\tan\\theta = \\mu_s$. A sliding block is slowed by kinetic friction alone, giving retardation $\\frac{f_k}{m} = \\mu_k g$. On a level turn, $\\frac{mv^2}{R} \\le \\mu_s mg$, so $v_{max} = \\sqrt{\\mu_s R g}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A train moves along a straight horizontal track with an acceleration of $3\\ m/s^2$. A stone of mass 0.2 kg is dropped from its window. Neglecting air resistance and taking $g = 10\\ m/s^2$, what is the magnitude of the net force on the stone just after it is released?","image":"","options":["2.0 N","2.09 N","0.6 N","2.6 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Once released, the stone has no horizontal force and keeps no memory of the train's acceleration. Only gravity acts: $F = mg = 0.2 \\times 10 = 2$ N. Combining it with $ma = 0.6$ N is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.1 kg is dropped from rest from a height of 5 m onto a hard floor. It rebounds to a height of 1.8 m and stays in contact with the floor for 0.01 s. What is the average normal force exerted by the floor on the ball during the contact? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ and neglect air resistance.)","image":"","options":["160 N","161 N","159 N","40 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ball hits the floor at 10 m/s downward and leaves at 6 m/s upward, so $\\Delta p = 0.1 \\times 16 = 1.6$ N s and the average net force is 160 N upward. The normal force must also balance the weight, so $N = 160 + mg = 161$ N. Answering 160 N forgets gravity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 2 kg is held against a rough vertical wall by pressing it with a horizontal force F applied perpendicular to the wall. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the wall is 0.4. What is the minimum value of F that keeps the block from slipping down? (Take g = 10 m/s^2)","image":"","options":["50 N","8 N","20 N","25 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The normal reaction from the wall equals F. Friction acts upward and can be at most $\\mu_s F$. To hold the block we need $\\mu_s F \\ge mg$, so $F_{min} = \\frac{mg}{\\mu_s} = \\frac{20}{0.4} = 50$ N. A common trap is to write the friction as $\\mu_s mg$ (8 N), even though the normal force here is F.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A rifle of mass 5 kg, initially at rest, fires a bullet of mass 20 g horizontally with a speed of 400 m/s. What is the recoil speed of the rifle?","image":"","options":["1.6 m/s","0.8 m/s","2.0 m/s","16 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The rifle and bullet start at rest, so their total momentum stays zero: $m_b v_b = M V$. This gives $V = \\frac{0.02 \\times 400}{5} = 1.6$ m/s. A common trap is forgetting to convert 20 g to 0.02 kg.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 4 kg mass hangs from the ceiling by a light rope. A horizontal force of 30 N is applied at a point on the rope above the mass, and the system comes to rest. What is the tension in the upper part of the rope (between that point and the ceiling)? Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$.","image":"","options":["50 N","70 N","40 N","10 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The lower segment of the rope carries 40 N, the weight of the mass. At the point where the force acts, the upper tension must balance 40 N vertically and 30 N horizontally, so $T = \\sqrt{40^2 + 30^2} = 50$ N. Adding the two forces directly (70 N) ignores their vector nature.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A particle of mass 0.5 kg moves along the x-axis. Its position is given by $x = 2t^3 - 3t^2 + 4$, where x is in metres and t in seconds. What is the net force on the particle at t = 2 s?","image":"","options":["9 N","6 N","18 N","12 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiating gives $v = 6t^2 - 6t$ and $a = 12t - 6$. At t = 2 s, a = 18 m/s^2, so $F = ma = 0.5 \\times 18 = 9$ N. The distractors come from using the velocity (12 m/s) or from forgetting to multiply by the mass.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A small ball rests on a perfectly smooth (frictionless) horizontal floor of a train compartment. The train is initially moving with uniform velocity. The train then starts accelerating forward with acceleration $a$. Which description of the ball's subsequent motion (before it hits a wall) is correct?","image":"","options":["Relative to the ground it continues with the original uniform velocity; relative to the train it moves backward with acceleration $a$","Relative to the ground it accelerates backward with acceleration $a$","Relative to the ground it accelerates forward with acceleration $a$, since it is inside the train","It remains at rest relative to the train, since inertia keeps it moving with the train"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"With no friction, no horizontal external force acts on the ball, so by the first law its ground-frame velocity stays constant. The floor accelerates forward beneath it, so relative to the train it appears to accelerate backward.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The momentum p of a body of constant mass varies with time t in four cases (p in SI units, t in s). Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) $p = $ constant\n(B) $p = 4t + 2$\n(C) $p = 3t^2$\n(D) $p = t^3$\nList-II\n(I) Force proportional to $t^2$\n(II) Zero force\n(III) Force proportional to t\n(IV) Constant non-zero force","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Using $F = \\frac{dp}{dt}$: (A) gives 0, (B) gives 4 (constant), (C) gives 6t and (D) gives $3t^2$. The constant term in (B) does not contribute to the force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 2 kg travels according to the law $x(t) = pt + qt^2 + rt^3$ where $p = 3$ m s$^{-1}$, $q = 4$ m s$^{-2}$ and $r = 5$ m s$^{-3}$. The force acting on the body at t = 2 seconds is","image":"","options":["136 N","134 N","158 N","68 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiating twice gives $a = 2q + 6rt = 8 + 30t$, which equals 68 m s$^{-2}$ at t = 2 s. The force is $F = ma = 2 \\times 68 = 136$ N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two blocks of masses 4 kg and 2 kg are in contact on a smooth horizontal surface. A horizontal force of 30 N pushes the 4 kg block, which in turn pushes the 2 kg block ahead of it. What force does the 2 kg block exert on the 4 kg block?","image":"","options":["10 N, opposite to the applied force","20 N, opposite to the applied force","30 N, opposite to the applied force","15 N, opposite to the applied force"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The common acceleration is 30/6 = 5 m/s^2, so the 4 kg block pushes the 2 kg block with 2 × 5 = 10 N forward. By the third law, the 2 kg block pushes back on the 4 kg block with 10 N, opposite to the applied force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following sets of concurrent forces (magnitudes in newton) can keep a particle in equilibrium?\n(2, 3, 4), (1, 2, 4), (5, 5, 5), (3, 4, 8), (6, 6, 12)","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Three forces balance only if each is at most the sum of the other two, so that they can close a triangle. The sets (2, 3, 4), (5, 5, 5) and (6, 6, 12) satisfy this; (6, 6, 12) works with the forces collinear. The sets (1, 2, 4) and (3, 4, 8) fail.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The momentum of a particle varies with time as $\\vec{p} = (2t^2\\,\\hat{i} + 3t\\,\\hat{j})$ kg m/s, with t in seconds. What is the magnitude of the net force on the particle at t = 1 s?","image":"","options":["5 N","7 N","$\\sqrt{13}$ N","4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The force is $\\vec{F} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt} = 4t\\,\\hat{i} + 3\\,\\hat{j}$. At t = 1 s this is $4\\hat{i} + 3\\hat{j}$, with magnitude 5 N. The value $\\sqrt{13}$ is the magnitude of the momentum itself, and 7 N comes from adding the components as scalars.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A man of mass 60 kg stands on a boat of mass 240 kg that is at rest on still water. He jumps horizontally off the boat with a speed of 5 m/s relative to the boat. Neglect water resistance. What is the speed of the boat relative to the water just after the jump?","image":"","options":["1 m/s","1.25 m/s","0.8 m/s","5 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the boat recoil with speed $V$. The man's ground speed is then $5 - V$, and momentum conservation gives $60(5 - V) = 240V$, so $V = 1$ m/s. Treating 5 m/s as the man's speed relative to the ground wrongly gives 1.25 m/s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block rests on a rough horizontal floor with $\\mu_s = \\mu_k = 0.3$. Starting at $t = 0$, a horizontal force $F = 6t$ (F in N, t in s) is applied to it. What is the speed of the block at $t = 3$ s? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["6 m/s","4.5 m/s","13.5 m/s","3 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Limiting friction is $0.3 \\times 20 = 6$ N, so the block stays at rest until $6t = 6$, i.e. $t = 1$ s. From 1 s to 3 s the net impulse is $\\int_1^3 (6t - 6)\\,dt = 12$ N s, giving $v = 6$ m/s. Subtracting friction over the full 3 s gives 4.5 m/s, which is wrong because static friction only matches the applied force before motion starts.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A cyclist moving at 36 km/h on a level road wants to take a circular turn without reducing speed. The coefficient of static friction between the tyres and the road is 0.25. What is the minimum radius of the turn the cyclist can take without slipping? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["40 m","4 m","518.4 m","160 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First convert the speed: 36 km/h = 10 m/s. The condition $v^2 \\le \\mu_s R g$ gives $R_{min} = \\frac{v^2}{\\mu_s g} = \\frac{100}{2.5} = 40$ m. The value 518.4 m comes from using the speed in km/h without converting.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A wooden block of mass 3 kg rests on a soft floor. An iron cylinder of mass 12 kg is placed on top of it. The floor yields steadily, so the block and cylinder move down together with an acceleration of 0.2 m/s^2. Take g = 10 m/s^2. What is the magnitude of the force exerted by the cylinder on the block?","image":"","options":["117.6 N","120 N","122.4 N","147 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For the cylinder, 120 − N = 12 × 0.2, so N = 117.6 N upward on the cylinder by the block. By the third law, the cylinder pushes down on the block with 117.6 N. The value 147 N is the floor's normal force on the whole system, not the force between the cylinder and the block.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.2 kg moves at 2 m/s towards a bat. The force-time graph of the force exerted by the bat on the ball, directed away from the bat, is a triangle. The force rises linearly from 0 to 600 N in the first 2 ms and then falls linearly back to 0 at 4 ms. Neglecting gravity during the contact, what is the speed of the ball just after it leaves the bat?","image":"","options":["4 m/s","6 m/s","8 m/s","2 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The impulse equals the area under the force-time graph: $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 600 \\times 0.004 = 1.2$ N s, which gives a velocity change of 6 m/s away from the bat. Starting from 2 m/s towards the bat, the final velocity is $-2 + 6 = 4$ m/s away. Answering 6 m/s ignores the initial velocity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block is held against a rough vertical wall by pressing it with a horizontal force F. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the wall is 0.5. What is the minimum value of F that keeps the block from sliding down? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["10 N","20 N","40 N","80 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The normal reaction from the wall equals F, and friction must balance the weight: $\\mu_s F \\geq mg$. This gives $F_{min} = \\frac{mg}{\\mu_s} = \\frac{20}{0.5} = 40$ N. The trap is to multiply by $\\mu_s$ instead of dividing.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a projectile moving under gravity alone, the horizontal component of its velocity stays constant.\nStatement II: A force that is not parallel to a body's velocity changes only the component of velocity along the direction of the force.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The second law is a vector law, so a force changes only the velocity component along its own direction. Gravity is vertical, so the horizontal velocity of a projectile is unchanged. Both statements are therefore correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a box lying on the floor of an accelerating truck remains at rest relative to the truck, the frictional force on the box acts in the direction of the truck's acceleration.\nReason (R): Friction opposes impending or actual relative motion between the surfaces in contact, not motion itself.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Without friction, the box would slide backward relative to the floor. Static friction opposes this impending relative motion by acting forward, and it is the only horizontal force that can accelerate the box along with the truck. R therefore correctly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Impulse is a vector quantity in the direction of the change in momentum.\n(ii) A given impulse delivered in a shorter time requires a larger average force.\n(iii) The impulse given to a ball by a bat is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the impulse given to the bat by the ball.\n(iv) Newtonian mechanics treats impulsive forces as fundamentally different from ordinary forces.\n(v) During the brief action of an impulsive force, the change in position of the body can be neglected.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statements (i), (ii), (iii) and (v) are correct. Statement (iii) holds because the third-law forces are equal and opposite and act for the same contact time. Statement (iv) is false, since Newtonian mechanics treats an impulsive force like any other force that is simply large and short-lived.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball strikes a smooth rigid wall with its velocity making an angle of 30° with the normal to the wall. It rebounds with the same speed at 30° on the other side of the normal.\nStatement I: The force exerted by the ball on the wall is directed along the normal to the wall, into the wall.\nStatement II: The component of the ball's momentum parallel to the wall does not change during the collision.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only the normal component of momentum reverses, so the impulse on the ball lies along the normal, pointing away from the wall. By the third law, the force on the wall is normal and into the wall. The common wrong idea is that the force on the wall is inclined at 30° to the normal.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following bodies have a non-zero net force acting on them at the instant described?\n(i) A car moving with constant velocity on a rough straight road\n(ii) A stone whirled at uniform speed in a horizontal circle by a string\n(iii) A ball thrown vertically upward, at its highest point\n(iv) A raindrop falling with constant speed\n(v) A stone just after it is released from the window of an accelerating train\n(vi) A projectile at the highest point of its path","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cases (ii), (iii), (v) and (vi) have a non-zero net force: the centripetal tension in (ii) and gravity alone in the other three. In (i) and (iv) the velocity is constant, so the net force is zero. Note that momentary rest in (iii) does not mean zero force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Two blocks of unequal masses are in contact on a smooth floor. When they are pushed by a given horizontal force, the contact force between them is larger if the force is applied to the lighter block than if it is applied to the heavier block.\nReason (R): The contact force alone accelerates the block that is not directly pushed, so it equals that block's mass times the common acceleration.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The common acceleration is the same in both cases. The contact force equals the mass of the unpushed block times this acceleration. When the lighter block is pushed, the unpushed block is the heavier one, so the contact force is larger. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly states the Aristotelian law of motion as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["An external force is required to keep a body in motion","A body at rest stays at rest unless acted on by a net external force","The rate of change of momentum is proportional to the applied force","A body in motion comes to rest only because of friction"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Aristotle held that something external must keep pushing a moving body, such as air pushing an arrow. The other options are Newtonian ideas that replaced his view.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A small stone of mass 0.5 kg is tied to a light string and whirled in a vertical circle of radius 1 m. At the lowest point of the circle its speed is 4 m/s. What is the tension in the string at that instant? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["13 N","8 N","3 N","5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the lowest point the net upward (centripetal) force is $T - mg = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$. So $T = 5 + \\frac{0.5 \\times 16}{1} = 13$ N. Subtracting the weight instead of adding it gives the trap answer of 3 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Blocks of masses 2 kg and 3 kg are in contact on a rough horizontal floor. The coefficient of kinetic friction is 0.2 for both blocks. A horizontal force of 30 N pushes the 2 kg block, and both blocks slide together (g = 10 m/s$^2$).\nStatement I: The contact force between the blocks is 18 N.\nStatement II: When both blocks have the same coefficient of kinetic friction, the contact force while sliding is the same as it would be on a frictionless floor with the same applied force.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The total friction is 10 N, so $a = \\frac{30-10}{5} = 4$ m/s$^2$. For the 3 kg block, the contact force is $N = 3(4) + 0.2(30) = 18$ N. In general, $N = m_2(a + \\mu g) = \\frac{m_2 F}{m_1+m_2}$, which is the frictionless result, so both statements hold.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A girl of mass 50 kg stands on a weighing scale (calibrated in newtons) placed on the floor of a lift. The lift is moving downward with a uniform acceleration of $3\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$. What does the scale read? (Take $g = 10\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["350 N","500 N","650 N","150 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take downward as positive for the girl: $mg - N = ma$, so $N = m(g - a) = 50 \\times 7 = 350$ N. The scale shows the normal force N, not the weight. Adding the accelerations (650 N) would be correct only for upward acceleration.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stone tied to the end of a string is whirled round in a horizontal circle. Its speed is increased beyond the maximum value the string can withstand, and the string breaks suddenly. Which of the following correctly describes the trajectory of the stone after the string breaks?","image":"","options":["the stone moves radially outwards","the stone flies off tangentially from the instant the string breaks","the stone flies off at an angle with the tangent whose magnitude depends on the speed of the particle","the stone moves along a spiral path, gradually moving away from the centre"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Once the string breaks, no horizontal force acts on the stone. By the first law, it continues with the velocity it had at that instant, which is along the tangent to the circle.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.22, made self-contained, 4th option added)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 2 kg is pressed against a rough vertical wall by a horizontal force of 80 N. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the wall is 0.4. The block remains at rest. What is the magnitude of the frictional force exerted by the wall on the block? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["20 N","32 N","50 N","12 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The normal force is 80 N, so the limiting friction is $\\mu_s N = 32$ N. Static friction is self-adjusting, so it only needs to balance the weight, $mg = 20$ N, which is less than this limit. The trap is writing $f_s = \\mu_s N$ when the limiting value is not actually reached.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A shell of mass 0.020 kg is fired by a gun of mass 100 kg. If the muzzle speed of the shell is 80 m s$^{-1}$, what is the recoil speed of the gun?","image":"","options":["0.16 m s$^{-1}$","1.6 m s$^{-1}$","0.016 m s$^{-1}$","0.0016 m s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The total momentum is zero both before and after firing, so 100 V = 0.020 × 80. This gives V = 0.016 m s$^{-1}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.19, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A small bob of mass 0.2 kg hangs by a light string from the roof of a truck. The truck moves on a straight level road with a constant acceleration of 7.5 m/s$^2$, and the bob stays at rest relative to the truck with the string inclined to the vertical. What is the tension in the string? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["2.0 N","3.5 N","1.5 N","2.5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In the ground frame, $T\\sin\\theta = ma$ and $T\\cos\\theta = mg$. This gives $T = m\\sqrt{g^2 + a^2} = 0.2 \\times 12.5 = 2.5$ N. Answering 2.0 N wrongly assumes the tension equals mg.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.2 kg strikes a rigid smooth wall with speed 10 m/s. Its direction of motion makes an angle of 60° with the normal to the wall, and it rebounds with the same speed at the same angle. The contact lasts 0.01 s. What is the magnitude of the average force exerted by the wall on the ball?","image":"","options":["100 N","200 N","346.4 N","400 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only the normal component of momentum reverses, so $|\\Delta p| = 2mu\\cos 60^\\circ = 2$ N s. The average force is $2/0.01 = 200$ N, directed along the normal. Using $\\sin 60^\\circ$ gives 346.4 N, and ignoring the angle gives 400 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 4 kg block at rest on a horizontal floor is pushed with a horizontal force of 25 N. For the block and floor, $\\mu_s = 0.5$ and $\\mu_k = 0.3$. What is the acceleration of the block? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["0 m/s$^2$","1.25 m/s$^2$","3.25 m/s$^2$","6.25 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The limiting static friction is $0.5 \\times 40 = 20$ N, which is less than 25 N, so the block slides. Once it moves, kinetic friction ($0.3 \\times 40 = 12$ N) acts. The acceleration is $\\frac{25 - 12}{4} = 3.25$ m/s$^2$. Using $\\mu_s$ instead gives the wrong value of 1.25 m/s$^2$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A shell is fired from the ground with a speed of $20\\sqrt{2}$ m/s at $45^\\circ$ to the horizontal. At the highest point of its path, it explodes into two fragments of equal mass. One fragment retraces its path back to the point of firing. What is the speed of the other fragment just after the explosion? Neglect air resistance.","image":"","options":["60 m/s","40 m/s","20 m/s","80 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the top, the shell's velocity is purely horizontal: $20\\sqrt{2}\\cos 45^\\circ = 20$ m/s. The fragment that retraces its path must move at 20 m/s backward. Conservation of momentum during the brief explosion gives $m(20) = \\frac{m}{2}(-20) + \\frac{m}{2}v$, so $v = 60$ m/s. Answering 40 m/s results from ignoring that the first fragment moves backward.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A bomb at rest explodes into two fragments whose masses are in the ratio 1 : 3. What is the ratio of the speed of the lighter fragment to the speed of the heavier fragment?","image":"","options":["3 : 1","1 : 3","1 : 1","9 : 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The total momentum stays zero, so $m_1 v_1 = m_2 v_2$ and the speeds are inversely proportional to the masses. The lighter piece therefore moves 3 times faster. The ratio 9 : 1 would be the ratio of kinetic energies, not speeds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stone of mass 0.5 kg is falling freely near the earth's surface (take g = 10 m/s^2). What force does the stone exert on the earth?","image":"","options":["Zero, since the stone is not in contact with the earth","5 N, directed upward toward the stone","5 N, directed downward toward the earth's centre","A force much smaller than 5 N, because the earth is far more massive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By the third law, the stone pulls the earth with a force equal and opposite to the earth's pull on it, so the force is 5 N toward the stone. Contact is not needed. The earth's huge mass only makes its resulting acceleration negligible; it does not make the force smaller.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A car starting from rest on a straight road picks up speed because of the frictional force exerted by the road on its tyres.\nReason (R): Internal forces of a system cannot change the velocity of the system as a whole.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Engine forces are internal to the car, and internal forces cancel in pairs. So the car's acceleration must come from an external force along the road, which is friction. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg block rests on a rough horizontal table ($\\mu_s = 0.4$). It is tied by a light string, passing over a smooth pulley at the table's edge, to a hanging block of mass 1.5 kg. The system is released from rest. What is the magnitude of the frictional force acting on the 5 kg block? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["15 N","20 N","0 N","12 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The limiting static friction is $0.4 \\times 50 = 20$ N, which exceeds the 15 N pull of the hanging block, so the system stays at rest. Static friction is self-adjusting and only balances the actual pull, giving 15 N. Writing $f_s = \\mu_s N$ without checking is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg block rests on a rough horizontal table, with a coefficient of kinetic friction of 0.2 between the block and the table. It is connected by a light inextensible string, passing over a smooth pulley at the table's edge, to a 3 kg block hanging freely. The system is released and starts moving. What is the tension in the string? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["22.5 N","18.75 N","30 N","10 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The system acceleration is $a = \\frac{30 - 0.2 \\times 50}{8} = 2.5$ m/s$^2$. For the hanging block, $T = 3(10 - 2.5) = 22.5$ N. Ignoring friction gives 18.75 N, and taking the tension equal to the hanging weight (30 N) wrongly treats the system as being in equilibrium.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A constant force acting on a body of mass 3.0 kg changes its speed from 2.0 m s$^{-1}$ to 3.5 m s$^{-1}$ in 25 s. The direction of the motion of the body remains unchanged. What is the magnitude of the force?","image":"","options":["0.06 N","0.24 N","0.18 N","0.42 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The acceleration is (3.5 - 2.0)/25 = 0.06 m s$^{-2}$. The force is F = 3.0 × 0.06 = 0.18 N, directed along the motion.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.6, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"For a car taking a circular turn of radius R on a road banked at angle $\\theta$, with coefficient of static friction $\\mu_s$ between the tyres and the road, which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["If the car moves slower than the optimum speed $v_o = \\sqrt{Rg\\tan\\theta}$, the static friction on it acts up the slope","The car can remain parked at rest on the banked road only if $\\tan\\theta \\le \\mu_s$","For the same $\\mu_s$ and R, the maximum safe speed on the banked road is greater than on a level road","At the optimum speed, static friction reaches its limiting value $\\mu_s N$ and acts down the slope"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"At the optimum speed, the horizontal component of the normal force alone provides the centripetal force, so friction is zero, not limiting. Below $v_o$, the car tends to slide down, so friction acts up the slope. A parked car stays put only if $\\tan\\theta \\le \\mu_s$, and banking raises $v_{max}$ above the level-road value $\\sqrt{\\mu_s Rg}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stationary object of mass 4 kg explodes into three fragments. A 1 kg fragment moves along the +x direction at 12 m/s. Another 1 kg fragment moves along the +y direction at 16 m/s. What is the speed of the remaining 2 kg fragment?","image":"","options":["10 m/s","20 m/s","14 m/s","28 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The total momentum must remain zero. The third fragment's momentum therefore has magnitude $\\sqrt{12^2+16^2} = 20$ kg m/s, so its speed is $\\frac{20}{2} = 10$ m/s. Choosing 20 m/s mistakes the momentum magnitude for the speed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A bullet of mass 10 g moving horizontally at 300 m/s strikes a wooden block of mass 2.99 kg. The block is at rest on a frictionless horizontal surface, and the bullet gets embedded in it. What is the common velocity of the block and bullet just after impact?","image":"","options":["1 m/s","0.5 m/s","100 m/s","3 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Momentum is conserved during the impact: $0.01 \\times 300 = (0.01 + 2.99)v$, so $v = 1$ m/s. The combined mass must be used, not just the block's mass. Momentum is conserved even though this collision is inelastic.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.2 kg moving horizontally at 15 m/s strikes a vertical wall normally. It rebounds along the same line with the same speed. What is the magnitude of the impulse imparted to the ball by the wall?","image":"","options":["6 N s","3 N s","0 N s","12 N s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The momentum reverses direction, so $\\Delta p = mv - (-mv) = 2mv = 2 \\times 0.2 \\times 15 = 6$ N s. Answering 0 is the trap: it treats momentum as a scalar and ignores the reversal of direction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 2 kg is initially at rest on a smooth horizontal surface. A horizontal force that varies with time as $F = 6t$ (F in newton, t in second) acts on it. What is the speed of the body at $t = 2$ s?","image":"","options":["6 m/s","3 m/s","12 m/s","24 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The impulse is the area under the F–t graph: $\\int_0^2 6t\\,dt = 3t^2\\big|_0^2 = 12$ N s. This equals the change in momentum, so $v = 12/2 = 6$ m/s. The trap is to use the final force of 12 N as if it were constant, which gives 12 m/s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A seasoned cricketer draws his hands backward while catching a fast-moving ball. The main effect of doing this is that it:","image":"","options":["reduces the change in momentum of the ball","increases the impulse delivered to the hands","increases the time over which the ball is stopped, so a smaller average force is needed","makes the ball's final momentum non-zero, so less force is required"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The change in momentum (and hence the impulse) is fixed, because the ball must be brought to rest. Spreading this change over a longer time lowers the average force $F = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The SI unit of impulse, N s, is equivalent to which of the following?","image":"","options":["kg m s$^{-2}$","kg m s$^{-1}$","kg m$^2$ s$^{-1}$","N s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Impulse equals change in momentum, so its unit is that of momentum: N s = (kg m s$^{-2}$)(s) = kg m s$^{-1}$. The unit kg m s$^{-2}$ is the unit of force, not impulse.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a car takes a circular turn on a level road, the centripetal force is provided by kinetic (sliding) friction between the tyres and the road.\nStatement II: The frictional force on the car opposes the impending motion of the car away from the circle.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The tyres do not slide on the road, so the centripetal force is provided by static friction, not kinetic friction. Static friction opposes the impending motion of the car away from the circle, so Statement II is correct.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A circular track of radius 100 m is banked at an angle $\\theta$ such that $\\tan\\theta = 0.25$. The coefficient of static friction between the tyres and the track is 0.5. What is the maximum speed at which a car can go round the track without skidding? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["29.3 m/s","27.4 m/s","15.8 m/s","22.4 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Using $v_{max} = \\left[Rg\\frac{\\mu_s + \\tan\\theta}{1 - \\mu_s\\tan\\theta}\\right]^{1/2} = \\sqrt{1000 \\times \\frac{0.75}{0.875}} \\approx 29.3$ m/s. The distractors come from three mistakes: dropping the denominator gives 27.4, ignoring friction gives 15.8, and ignoring banking gives 22.4.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.15 kg moving at 20 m/s is struck by a bat so that it moves off at right angles to its original direction. Its speed after the hit is still 20 m/s. What is the magnitude of the impulse imparted to the ball?","image":"","options":["0 N s","3.0 N s","4.24 N s","6.0 N s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The initial and final momenta each have magnitude 3 N s and are perpendicular. So $|\\Delta \\vec{p}| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 3^2} = 3\\sqrt{2} \\approx 4.24$ N s. An unchanged speed does not mean zero impulse, because the direction of the momentum has changed.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): On a level circular road, a loaded truck and a light car with the same tyre–road coefficient of static friction have the same maximum speed for safe turning on a given curve.\nReason (R): Both the centripetal force required and the maximum static friction available are proportional to the mass of the vehicle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The condition is $\\frac{mv^2}{R} \\le \\mu_s mg$. Mass cancels from both sides, leaving $v_{max} = \\sqrt{\\mu_s R g}$. So R correctly explains why A holds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A man of mass 70 kg stands on a weighing scale in a lift. What are the readings on the scale when the lift is moving (b) downwards with a uniform acceleration of 5 m s$^{-2}$, and (c) upwards with a uniform acceleration of 5 m s$^{-2}$? Give the answer as (reading in (b), reading in (c)) in newtons. (Take g = 10 m s$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["(350 N, 1050 N)","(1050 N, 350 N)","(700 N, 700 N)","(700 N, 1050 N)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The scale reads the normal force R. Accelerating down, R = m(g - a) = 70 × 5 = 350 N (35 kg-wt). Accelerating up, R = m(g + a) = 70 × 15 = 1050 N (105 kg-wt).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.13, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A student of mass 50 kg stands on a weighing scale (calibrated in newtons) kept on the floor of a lift. The lift moves upward with a uniform acceleration of 2 m/s$^2$. What does the scale read? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["400 N","500 N","600 N","100 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For the student, $R - mg = ma$, so $R = m(g+a) = 50 \\times 12 = 600$ N. The scale shows R, not the weight mg. Picking 400 N comes from subtracting the acceleration instead of adding it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A car of mass m starts from rest and acquires a velocity along east $\\vec{v} = v\\hat{i}$ (v > 0) in two seconds. Assuming the car moves with uniform acceleration, the force exerted on the car is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{mv}{2}$ eastward and is exerted by the car engine.","$\\frac{mv}{2}$ eastward and is due to the friction on the tyres exerted by the road.","more than $\\frac{mv}{2}$ eastward exerted due to the engine and overcomes the friction of the road.","$\\frac{mv}{2}$ exerted by the engine."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The acceleration is v/2, so the net external force is mv/2 eastward. The engine is internal to the car; the external forward force comes from the road's friction on the tyres.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The maximum speed with which a car can safely negotiate a level circular road is the same for a light car and a heavy truck, if $\\mu_s$ and the radius are the same.\nReason (R): Both the required centripetal force and the maximum static friction available are proportional to the mass of the vehicle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The no-skid condition is $\\frac{mv^2}{R} \\le \\mu_s m g$. Mass cancels from both sides, giving $v_{max} = \\sqrt{\\mu_s R g}$. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A spring balance hangs from the ceiling of a lift and a body is suspended from it. When the lift is at rest, the balance reads 40 N. The lift is now moving downward with a constant speed of 3 m/s. What is the reading of the balance? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["28 N","40 N","52 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At constant velocity the acceleration is zero, so the net force on the body is zero by the first law. The spring force therefore still equals the weight, 40 N. The speed of 3 m/s is irrelevant.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Consider Galileo's double inclined plane experiment with ideal, frictionless planes.\nStatement I: A ball released from rest on one plane rises to the same height on the second plane, whatever the slope of the second plane.\nStatement II: If the second plane is made horizontal, the ball still comes to rest after travelling a finite distance.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Without friction the ball regains its initial height, travelling a longer distance on a gentler slope. On a horizontal second plane it can never regain that height, so it moves on forever with constant velocity. This idealised result is the law of inertia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 2 kg moves in a straight line. A constant force acting along the direction of motion increases its speed from 3 m/s to 8 m/s in 0.5 s. What is the magnitude of the force?","image":"","options":["20 N","10 N","22 N","32 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The change in momentum is $2\\times(8-3) = 10$ kg m/s, and $F = \\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{10}{0.5} = 20$ N. A common trap is to use the final momentum or the final speed alone instead of the change in momentum.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stationary object of mass 3 kg explodes into three equal pieces. Two of the pieces fly off at 30 m/s each, in directions perpendicular to each other. What is the speed of the third piece?","image":"","options":["42.4 m/s","30 m/s","60 m/s","21.2 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The vector sum of momenta must be zero, so the third piece's momentum cancels the resultant of the two perpendicular momenta. Each piece has mass 1 kg, so $v_3 = \\sqrt{30^2 + 30^2} \\approx 42.4$ m/s. Its direction is opposite to the bisector of the first two directions. Adding speeds as scalars (60 m/s) is the trap.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Newton's third law of motion is correct?","image":"","options":["Action always comes first and causes the reaction a moment later","Action and reaction act on the same body, so they always cancel each other","The force on body A by body B is equal and opposite to the force on B by A, and the two act at the same instant","Action-reaction pairs exist only for contact forces, not for forces acting at a distance such as gravity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The third law says mutual forces between two bodies are equal, opposite and simultaneous, with no cause-effect order between them. They act on different bodies, so they do not cancel for either body. The law holds for gravity too: a falling stone pulls the earth with an equal and opposite force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A small block rests on the smooth (frictionless) inclined face of a wedge. The face is inclined at $30^\\circ$ to the horizontal. The wedge is placed on a horizontal floor. It is pushed horizontally so that its inclined face moves towards the block. With what acceleration must the wedge move so that the block stays at rest relative to the wedge? (Take $g = 10\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$5.77\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$","$17.3\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$","$5.00\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$","$8.66\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the ground frame, only gravity and the normal force N act on the block, and the block shares the wedge's horizontal acceleration a. So $N\\cos\\theta = mg$ and $N\\sin\\theta = ma$, giving $a = g\\tan 30^\\circ \\approx 5.77\\ \\text{m s}^{-2}$. A common trap is using $\\sin\\theta$ (5.00) or $\\cot\\theta$ (17.3) instead of $\\tan\\theta$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg body hangs from a spring balance attached to the ceiling of a lift. At a certain instant the balance reads 40 N. Which of the following statements is correct? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["The lift must be moving downward","The lift is accelerating upward at 2 m/s$^2$","The lift has a downward acceleration of 2 m/s$^2$; it may be moving up while slowing down","The lift is moving with constant velocity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"From $mg - T = ma$, we get $a = (50 - 40)/5 = 2$ m/s$^2$ downward. The reading fixes only the acceleration, not the direction of velocity. A lift moving up and slowing down has exactly this acceleration.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A rocket with a lift-off mass 20,000 kg is blasted upwards with an initial acceleration of 5.0 m s$^{-2}$. Calculate the initial thrust (force) of the blast. (Take g = 10 m s$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ N","$2.0 \\times 10^{5}$ N","$3.0 \\times 10^{5}$ N","$4.0 \\times 10^{5}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The thrust must support the weight and also provide the upward acceleration: F - mg = ma. So F = m(g + a) = 20000 × 15 = 3.0 × 10$^5$ N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.9, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Three concurrent forces of magnitudes 3 N, 4 N and 5 N keep a particle in equilibrium. What is the angle between the 3 N and 4 N forces?","image":"","options":["$90^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$","$120^\\circ$","$180^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The resultant of the 3 N and 4 N forces must have magnitude 5 N. This gives $25 = 9 + 16 + 24\\cos\\theta$, so $\\cos\\theta = 0$ and $\\theta = 90^\\circ$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Consider an Atwood machine with a light inextensible string and a smooth, light pulley. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) The tension is the same throughout the string.\n(b) If the two masses are equal and the system is released from rest, it stays at rest.\n(c) If the masses are unequal, the tension equals the weight of the heavier mass.\n(d) If the masses are unequal, the tension lies between the weights of the two masses.\n(e) The acceleration of the masses does not depend on $g$.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statements (a), (b) and (d) are correct; a constant tension holds for a string of negligible mass. The tension is less than the heavier weight because the heavier mass accelerates downward, so (c) is wrong. The acceleration $\\frac{(m_2 - m_1)g}{m_1 + m_2}$ is proportional to $g$, so (e) is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 2 kg has acceleration $\\vec{a} = (3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$. What is the magnitude of the net force acting on it?","image":"","options":["7 N","10 N","14 N","5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$\\vec{F} = m\\vec{a} = (6\\hat{i} + 8\\hat{j})$ N, so $|\\vec{F}| = \\sqrt{36+64} = 10$ N. The value 14 N comes from wrongly adding the components as scalars.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A circular road of radius 100 m is banked at an angle $\\theta$ such that $\\tan\\theta = 0.4$. At what speed can a car take this turn so that no friction is needed at all? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["20 m/s","40 m/s","10 m/s","31.6 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the optimum speed, the horizontal component of the normal force alone provides the centripetal force. This gives $v_o = \\sqrt{R g \\tan\\theta} = \\sqrt{100 \\times 10 \\times 0.4} = 20$ m/s. The value 31.6 m/s wrongly leaves out $\\tan\\theta$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A bullet is fired into a wooden block resting on a frictionless horizontal surface. The bullet gets embedded in the block, and the two move off together.\nStatement I: The total momentum of the (bullet + block) system just after the impact equals the momentum of the bullet just before the impact.\nStatement II: The total kinetic energy of the (bullet + block) system just after the impact equals the kinetic energy of the bullet just before the impact.","image":"","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"There is no net horizontal external force, so momentum is conserved whether the collision is elastic or inelastic. When the bullet embeds in the block, the collision is inelastic and kinetic energy is not conserved, because some of it becomes heat and deformation.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two blocks of masses 2 kg and 3 kg are placed in contact on a smooth horizontal floor. A horizontal force of 20 N is applied to the 2 kg block, pushing it against the 3 kg block. What is the magnitude of the contact force between the blocks?","image":"","options":["8 N","12 N","20 N","4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The common acceleration is $\\frac{20}{5} = 4$ m/s$^2$. The contact force alone accelerates the 3 kg block, so it equals $3 \\times 4 = 12$ N. It is not the full 20 N, because part of the applied force accelerates the 2 kg block.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two masses 8 kg and 12 kg are connected at the two ends of a light inextensible string that goes over a frictionless pulley. Find the acceleration of the masses, and the tension in the string when the masses are released. Give the answer as (acceleration, tension). (Take g = 10 m s$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["(4.0 m s$^{-2}$, 80 N)","(10 m s$^{-2}$, 120 N)","(0.2 m s$^{-2}$, 100 N)","(2.0 m s$^{-2}$, 96 N)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The acceleration is $a = \\frac{(m_2 - m_1)g}{m_1 + m_2} = \\frac{4 \\times 10}{20} = 2.0$ m s$^{-2}$. The tension is $T = m_1(g + a) = 8 \\times 12 = 96$ N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.16, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following forces fundamentally arise from electrical forces between charged constituents of matter?\nNormal reaction, kinetic friction, tension in a string, buoyant force on an immersed solid, gravitational pull of the earth on a stone, viscous drag","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"All contact forces of mechanics ultimately trace back to electrical forces between nuclei and electrons. Normal reaction, friction, tension, buoyancy and viscous drag are all contact forces, which makes five. The earth's pull on the stone is gravitational and acts at a distance.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 2 kg is initially at rest on a smooth horizontal surface. A horizontal force acts on it as follows: the force rises linearly from 0 at t = 0 to 10 N at t = 4 s, and then stays constant at 10 N until t = 6 s. What is the speed of the body at t = 6 s?","image":"","options":["10 m/s","20 m/s","30 m/s","15 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Impulse equals the area under the force-time graph: $\\frac{1}{2}(4)(10) + 10 \\times 2 = 40$ N s. So $v = 40/2 = 20$ m/s. Taking 10 N for the whole 6 s wrongly gives 30 m/s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Masses of 3 kg and 2 kg hang over a light frictionless pulley fixed by a clamp, connected by a light inextensible string. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Acceleration of the masses\n(Q) Tension in the string\n(R) Force exerted by the clamp on the pulley\n(S) Net force on the 3 kg mass\nList-II\n(1) 24 N\n(2) 6 N\n(3) 2 m/s$^2$\n(4) 48 N","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The acceleration is $a = \\frac{(3-2)10}{5} = 2$ m/s$^2$ and the tension is $T = 3(10-2) = 24$ N. The clamp supports $2T = 48$ N, not the total weight of 50 N. The net force on the 3 kg mass is $3 \\times 2 = 6$ N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) The total momentum of an isolated system is conserved even though its particles exert forces on one another.\n(b) The law of conservation of momentum can be derived from Newton's second and third laws.\n(c) In an inelastic collision between two isolated bodies, the total momentum is not conserved.\n(d) When a body at rest explodes, the total kinetic energy of the fragments is zero.\n(e) The momentum of a stone falling freely under gravity is conserved.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only (a) and (b) are correct. Momentum is conserved in both elastic and inelastic collisions, so (c) is false. In (d), the total momentum of the fragments is zero, but their kinetic energy is positive. In (e), gravity is an external force on the stone, so its momentum is not conserved.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 4 kg block lies on a rough horizontal table, with coefficient of kinetic friction 0.25. It is connected by a light string, passing over a smooth pulley at the edge of the table, to a 2 kg block hanging vertically. The system moves. What is the tension in the string? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["16.7 N","20 N","13.3 N","10 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The friction force is $f_k = 0.25 \\times 40 = 10$ N, so $a = \\frac{20 - 10}{6} = \\frac{5}{3}$ m/s$^2$. For the hanging block, $T = 2(10 - \\frac{5}{3}) \\approx 16.7$ N. The tension is less than the hanging weight of 20 N because that block is accelerating downward.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Match the situation in List-I with the force that provides the required acceleration in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) A box at rest relative to the floor of an accelerating train\n(B) A stone whirled in a horizontal circle on a smooth table by a string\n(C) A car on a banked road moving at the optimum speed\n(D) The moon revolving around the earth\nList-II\n(P) Static friction\n(Q) Tension\n(R) Gravitational force\n(S) Horizontal component of normal reaction","image":"","options":["A-P, B-Q, C-S, D-R","A-S, B-Q, C-P, D-R","A-P, B-R, C-S, D-Q","A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the optimum speed on a banked road, no friction is needed and the horizontal component of N alone supplies the centripetal force. Static friction accelerates the box, string tension pulls the stone inward, and gravity holds the moon in orbit. Centripetal force is always some real material force, never an extra force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A passenger stands in a stationary bus. The bus starts suddenly, and the passenger is thrown backward. The correct reason is:","image":"","options":["The bus exerts a backward force on the passenger's upper body","Friction moves the feet forward with the bus, while the upper body tends to stay at rest due to inertia","Air inside the bus pushes the passenger backward","The passenger's weight acts backward when the bus accelerates"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Friction drags the feet along with the bus floor. The rest of the deformable body tends to remain where it was, so relative to the bus it falls backward. No backward force acts on the passenger.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Three blocks of masses 1 kg, 2 kg and 3 kg lie in a straight line on a smooth horizontal table. Light strings connect the 1 kg block to the 2 kg block, and the 2 kg block to the 3 kg block. A horizontal force of 12 N pulls the 3 kg block away from the others. What is the tension in the string between the 2 kg and 3 kg blocks?","image":"","options":["6 N","2 N","10 N","12 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The common acceleration is $\\frac{12}{6} = 2$ m/s$^2$. The string next to the 3 kg block must accelerate the 1 kg and 2 kg blocks together, so $T = (1+2) \\times 2 = 6$ N. The answer 2 N is the tension in the other string.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A block of mass m rests, relative to the wedge, on the smooth inclined face (angle $\\theta$) of a wedge that is accelerating horizontally with $a = g\\tan\\theta$. The normal force on the block is $mg\\cos\\theta$.\nStatement II: The normal force is a self-adjusting contact force; its value is fixed by the acceleration of the body through the second law, not by a fixed formula.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The block has no vertical acceleration, so $N\\cos\\theta = mg$, giving $N = \\frac{mg}{\\cos\\theta}$, which is larger than $mg\\cos\\theta$. Thus Statement I is wrong. Statement II is correct: the formula $mg\\cos\\theta$ holds only for a fixed incline, because N adjusts to whatever the motion requires.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball of mass 0.1 kg is dropped from rest from a height of 5 m onto a horizontal floor. It rebounds vertically to a height of 1.8 m. The ball is in contact with the floor for 0.02 s. Ignore air resistance and take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$. What is the magnitude of the average force exerted by the floor on the ball during the contact?","image":"","options":["81 N","80 N","79 N","20 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The ball hits the floor at $\\sqrt{2gh} = 10$ m/s downward and leaves at 6 m/s upward, so $\\Delta p = 0.1 \\times 16 = 1.6$ N s and the average net force is $\\frac{1.6}{0.02} = 80$ N upward. The floor must supply this net force plus balance the weight (1 N), giving 81 N. The trap is to take the net force as the floor's force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg block rests on a smooth incline of angle $30^\\circ$. It is connected by a light string, running parallel to the incline over a light, smooth pulley at the top, to a 3 kg block hanging vertically. The system is released. What is the magnitude of the total force exerted by the string on the pulley? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["31.2 N","36 N","18 N","25.5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The acceleration is $a = \\frac{30 - 2 \\times 10 \\sin 30^\\circ}{5} = 4$ m/s$^2$, so $T = 3(10 - 4) = 18$ N. The two string segments (down the incline and vertically down) make an angle of $60^\\circ$ at the pulley. Their resultant is therefore $2T\\cos 30^\\circ \\approx 31.2$ N, not simply $2T$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about friction is correct?","image":"","options":["The limiting value of static friction between two surfaces increases as the area of contact increases.","The coefficient of kinetic friction for a pair of surfaces is generally greater than the coefficient of static friction.","For the same weight, rolling friction is much smaller than static or sliding friction, possibly by 2 or 3 orders of magnitude.","A block resting on a rough horizontal table experiences a static frictional force even when no horizontal force is applied to it."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that rolling friction is far smaller than sliding or static friction for the same weight. That is why wheels and ball bearings reduce friction. Limiting friction does not depend on the area of contact, $\\mu_k$ is less than $\\mu_s$, and static friction is zero when no force is applied.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following bodies have zero net external force acting on them?\n(i) A car moving with constant speed on a circular track\n(ii) A ball at the highest point of its vertical upward throw\n(iii) A parachutist descending with constant velocity\n(iv) A book being pushed across a rough table with constant velocity\n(v) A stone at the instant it is released from a train moving with uniform velocity\n(vi) A spaceship far from all other bodies, rockets off, drifting with constant velocity","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Only (iii), (iv) and (vi) are unaccelerated, so they have zero net force. Case (i) has centripetal acceleration. In (ii) and (v), gravity acts even though the velocity may be momentarily zero or horizontal.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Placing ball bearings between the moving parts of a machine reduces the power lost as heat.\nReason (R): For the same weight, rolling friction is much smaller than static or sliding friction.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For the same load, rolling friction can be 2 to 3 orders of magnitude smaller than sliding friction. Ball bearings replace sliding with rolling, so less power is dissipated. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of static friction between the tyres of a scooter and a level road is 0.5. What is the maximum speed at which the scooter can take a circular turn of radius 20 m without skidding? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["10 m/s","14.1 m/s","7.07 m/s","100 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Using $v_{max} = \\sqrt{\\mu_s R g} = \\sqrt{0.5 \\times 20 \\times 10} = 10$ m/s. The value 100 comes from forgetting to take the square root.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 4 kg block on a rough horizontal table is tied by a light string, passing over a smooth pulley at the table's edge, to a hanging block of mass 6 kg. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the 4 kg block and the table is 0.5. While the system is moving, what is the tension in the string? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["36 N","60 N","40 N","24 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The kinetic friction is $0.5 \\times 40 = 20$ N, so $a = \\frac{60-20}{10} = 4$ m/s$^2$. For the hanging block, $T = 6(10-4) = 36$ N. The tension is less than the hanging weight because that block accelerates downward.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A particle in equilibrium must be at rest.\nReason (R): A particle is said to be in equilibrium when the net external force on it is zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Zero net external force means zero acceleration, so by the first law the particle is either at rest or moving with uniform velocity. Equilibrium therefore does not require rest.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 1 kg block A rests on a 4 kg plank B, which lies on a smooth horizontal floor. For the A–B surface, $\\mu_s = \\mu_k = 0.5$. A horizontal force of 30 N is applied to the plank B. What is the acceleration of the plank? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["6.25 m/s$^2$","6.0 m/s$^2$","7.5 m/s$^2$","5.0 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Friction can give A at most $\\mu g = 5$ m/s$^2$. Moving together would need $\\frac{30}{5} = 6$ m/s$^2$, so A slips. Kinetic friction of 5 N then acts backward on B, giving $a_B = \\frac{30 - 5}{4} = 6.25$ m/s$^2$. Choosing 6.0 m/s$^2$ wrongly assumes there is no slipping.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 10 kg lies on a rough horizontal floor with coefficient of static friction 0.75. A rope attached to the block can be pulled at any angle above the horizontal. What is the least possible magnitude of the pulling force that will just start the block moving? (Take g = 10 m/s^2)","image":"","options":["60 N","75 N","100 N","45 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If the rope is pulled at angle $\\alpha$ above the horizontal, the condition to start motion is $F\\cos\\alpha = \\mu_s(mg - F\\sin\\alpha)$. Minimising F gives $\\tan\\alpha = \\mu_s$ and $F_{min} = \\frac{\\mu_s mg}{\\sqrt{1+\\mu_s^2}} = \\frac{75}{1.25} = 60$ N. A purely horizontal pull would need 75 N, because an upward tilt reduces the normal force and hence the friction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A particle acted upon only by three concurrent forces of magnitudes 2 N, 3 N and 6 N cannot be in equilibrium.\nReason (R): Three concurrent forces keep a particle in equilibrium only if they can be represented by the sides of a closed triangle with arrows taken in the same sense.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For a closed triangle, no side can exceed the sum of the other two. Here 6 N is greater than 2 N + 3 N, so the three forces cannot form a closed triangle. R is therefore exactly the reason A holds.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg block rests on a rough horizontal table. The coefficient of static friction is $\\mu_s = 0.4$. A horizontal force of 12 N is applied to it, and the block stays at rest. What is the frictional force acting on the block? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["20 N","12 N","8 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The limiting static friction is $\\mu_s mg = 20$ N. The applied 12 N is less than this, so the block stays at rest. Static friction adjusts itself to equal the applied force, so it is 12 N. Writing $\\mu_s N$ (20 N) is the trap, because that is only the maximum possible value.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 10 kg block lies on a rough horizontal floor with $\\mu_s = 0.75$. A pulling force F is applied at an angle above the horizontal, and this angle can be chosen freely. What is the least magnitude of F that will just start the block moving? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["75 N","60 N","100 N","48 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pulling at angle $\\alpha$ gives the condition $F\\cos\\alpha = \\mu_s(mg - F\\sin\\alpha)$, so $F = \\frac{\\mu_s mg}{\\cos\\alpha + \\mu_s\\sin\\alpha}$. This is minimum when $\\tan\\alpha = \\mu_s$, giving $F_{min} = \\frac{\\mu_s mg}{\\sqrt{1+\\mu_s^2}} = \\frac{75}{1.25} = 60$ N. A horizontal pull would need the larger force of 75 N, because tilting the pull upward also reduces the normal force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 4 kg rests on a rough plane inclined at 30° to the horizontal. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the plane is 0.8. What is the magnitude of the frictional force acting on the block? (Take g = 10 m/s^2)","image":"","options":["20 N","27.7 N","34.6 N","32 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since $\\tan 30^\\circ \\approx 0.577 < 0.8$, the block stays at rest. Static friction therefore just balances the component of weight along the incline: $mg\\sin 30^\\circ = 20$ N. The value $\\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta \\approx 27.7$ N is only the maximum possible friction, which is not reached here.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A wooden block placed on a plank just begins to slide when the plank is tilted to an angle $\\theta_0$ with the horizontal. The block is replaced by another block of the same material and surface finish but three times the mass. At what angle will the new block just begin to slide?","image":"","options":["$\\theta_0$","$\\tan^{-1}(3\\tan\\theta_0)$","$\\frac{\\theta_0}{3}$","$\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\tan\\theta_0}{3}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the point of slipping, $mg\\sin\\theta = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta$, which gives $\\tan\\theta_{max} = \\mu_s$. The mass cancels, so the angle depends only on the surfaces in contact. The new block therefore starts to slide at the same angle $\\theta_0$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 5 kg block rests on a rough plane inclined at $37^\\circ$, where $\\sin 37^\\circ = 0.6$ and $\\cos 37^\\circ = 0.8$. The coefficient of static friction is $\\mu_s = 0.5$. A force F is applied parallel to the incline, directed up the slope. The block stays at rest for all F between $F_{min}$ and $F_{max}$. What is $F_{max} - F_{min}$? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["20 N","30 N","40 N","60 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Here $mg\\sin\\theta = 30$ N and the limiting friction is $\\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta = 20$ N. At $F_{min}$, friction acts up the slope, so $F_{min} = 30 - 20 = 10$ N. At $F_{max}$, friction acts down the slope, so $F_{max} = 30 + 20 = 50$ N. The difference is $2\\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta = 40$ N, because static friction reverses direction between the two limits.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two bodies A and B of masses 10 kg and 20 kg respectively kept on a smooth, horizontal surface are tied to the ends of a light string. A horizontal force F = 600 N is applied to (i) A, (ii) B along the direction of string. What is the tension in the string in each case? Give the answer as (tension in case (i), tension in case (ii)).","image":"","options":["(200 N, 400 N)","(600 N, 600 N)","(400 N, 200 N)","(300 N, 300 N)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Both bodies accelerate at 600/30 = 20 m s$^{-2}$. When A is pulled, the string accelerates B, so T = 20 × 20 = 400 N. When B is pulled, the string accelerates A, so T = 10 × 20 = 200 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.15, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A mass $m$ hangs from a ceiling by a light rope. A horizontal force $F$ is applied at some point P on the rope, and the system settles in equilibrium.\nStatement I: The angle that the upper segment of the rope makes with the vertical depends only on $F$ and $mg$, not on the length of the rope or the position of P.\nStatement II: The tension in the upper segment of the rope is equal to $mg$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Balancing forces at P gives $\\tan\\theta = F/mg$, which is independent of the rope length and the position of P. The upper tension is $\\sqrt{F^2 + (mg)^2}$, which is greater than $mg$; only the lower segment carries exactly $mg$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A circular track of radius 100 m is banked at an angle $\\theta$ with $\\tan\\theta = 0.5$. The coefficient of static friction between the tyres and the track is 0.5. What is the maximum speed at which a car can go round the track without skidding? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["36.5 m/s","31.6 m/s","28.3 m/s","22.4 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $v_{max} = \\left[\\frac{Rg(\\mu_s + \\tan\\theta)}{1 - \\mu_s\\tan\\theta}\\right]^{1/2} = \\left[\\frac{1000 \\times 1}{0.75}\\right]^{1/2} \\approx 36.5$ m/s. Two traps: dropping the denominator gives 31.6 m/s, and writing $1 + \\mu_s\\tan\\theta$ in the denominator gives 28.3 m/s.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the cable of a lift snaps and the lift falls freely, a person standing on a weighing scale inside it finds that the scale reads zero.\nReason (R): During free fall, the gravitational force on the person becomes zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In free fall the person and the scale both accelerate at g. From $mg - R = mg$ we get $R = 0$, so the reading is zero. Gravity still acts on the person with full value mg, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A ball is travelling with uniform translatory motion. This means that","image":"","options":["it is at rest.","the path can be a straight line or circular and the ball travels with uniform speed.","all parts of the ball have the same velocity (magnitude and direction) and the velocity is constant.","the centre of the ball moves with constant velocity and the ball spins about its centre uniformly."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In uniform translatory motion every particle of the body moves with the same velocity, and that velocity does not change with time. There is no rotation and no change in direction.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 10 kg rests on a rough plane inclined at 37° to the horizontal ($\\sin 37^\\circ = 0.6$, $\\cos 37^\\circ = 0.8$). The coefficient of static friction is 0.25. A force F is applied up the incline, parallel to its surface. What is the ratio of the force needed to just start the block moving up the incline to the minimum force needed to just prevent it from sliding down? (Take g = 10 m/s^2)","image":"","options":["2","3","1.5","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The component of weight along the incline is $mg\\sin\\theta = 60$ N, and the limiting friction is $\\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta = 20$ N. To just move the block up, friction acts down the slope, so $F_1 = 60 + 20 = 80$ N. To just hold it, friction acts up the slope, so $F_2 = 60 - 20 = 40$ N, giving a ratio of 2. The key step is recognising that friction reverses direction between the two cases.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A 2 kg mass is hung from the midpoint of a light string whose ends are fixed at the same horizontal level. In equilibrium, each half of the string makes an angle of $5^\\circ$ below the horizontal. What is the tension in the string? Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$ and $\\sin 5^\\circ = 0.0872$.","image":"","options":["114.7 N","57.4 N","10.0 N","229.5 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Only the vertical components of the two equal tensions support the weight, so $2T\\sin 5^\\circ = 20$ and $T \\approx 114.7$ N. As the sag angle approaches zero, the tension grows without limit, which is why such a string can never be made perfectly horizontal.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A cricket ball of mass 150 g has an initial velocity $\\vec{u} = (3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m s$^{-1}$ and a final velocity $\\vec{v} = -(3\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m s$^{-1}$ after being hit. The change in momentum (final momentum - initial momentum) is (in kg m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["zero","$-(0.45\\hat{i} + 0.6\\hat{j})$","$-(0.9\\hat{i} + 1.2\\hat{j})$","$-5(\\hat{i} + \\hat{j})$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The change in momentum is $\\Delta\\vec{p} = m(\\vec{v} - \\vec{u}) = 0.15 \\times (-6\\hat{i} - 8\\hat{j})$. This gives $-(0.9\\hat{i} + 1.2\\hat{j})$ kg m s$^{-1}$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Physics Class 11, Ch 5 (Q5.3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A shell of mass 3 kg moves horizontally at 20 m/s at the top of its flight. It explodes into two pieces of 1 kg and 2 kg. Just after the explosion, the 1 kg piece moves backward (opposite to the original motion) at 30 m/s. How much kinetic energy is released in the explosion?","image":"","options":["1875 J","2475 J","600 J","1275 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Momentum conservation gives $3 \\times 20 = 1 \\times (-30) + 2v$, so $v = 45$ m/s forward. The final kinetic energy is $\\frac{1}{2}(1)(30^2) + \\frac{1}{2}(2)(45^2) = 2475$ J and the initial kinetic energy is 600 J. The energy released is therefore 1875 J; 2475 J is only the final kinetic energy.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A body of mass 2 kg moves along the +x axis at 5 m/s. From t = 0, a force along the x-axis acts on it as follows:\n- the force is +6 N from t = 0 to t = 2 s;\n- it then decreases linearly to −6 N at t = 4 s, passing through zero at t = 3 s;\n- it stays at −6 N from t = 4 s to t = 6 s.\nWhat is the velocity of the body at t = 6 s?","image":"","options":["+5 m/s","+11 m/s","−1 m/s","+17 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Net impulse = +12 N s (0–2 s) + 3 − 3 N s (2–4 s, two equal triangles) − 12 N s (4–6 s) = 0. So the momentum, and hence the velocity, returns to +5 m/s. The trap is to add only the positive areas.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":169,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a rough plane is gradually tilted, a heavier block and a lighter block of the same material on the same surface begin to slide at the same angle of inclination.\nReason (R): At the angle where sliding just begins, $\\tan\\theta_{max} = \\mu_s$, and $\\mu_s$ depends only on the nature of the surfaces in contact.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the verge of sliding, $mg\\sin\\theta = \\mu_s mg\\cos\\theta$, so the mass cancels and $\\tan\\theta_{max} = \\mu_s$. Since $\\mu_s$ depends only on the surfaces, both blocks slip at the same angle. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":170,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Blocks A and B are joined by a light inextensible string and rest on a smooth horizontal table. A horizontal force F pulls block B, so that the string becomes taut and both blocks accelerate. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["In the free-body diagram of the system (A + B), the string tension does not appear because it is an internal force.","The tension acts on A and on B in the same direction.","The acceleration of A is $\\frac{F}{m_A}$.","The tension in the string is equal to F."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The forces the string exerts on A and on B are equal and opposite internal forces of the (A + B) system, so they cancel and are left out of its free-body diagram. Both blocks share the acceleration $\\frac{F}{m_A + m_B}$, and the tension $T = m_A a$ is less than F.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":171,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A rifle of mass 4 kg fires a bullet of mass 20 g horizontally with a speed of 400 m/s. The rifle is initially at rest and free to recoil. What is the recoil speed of the rifle?","image":"","options":["2 m/s","0.5 m/s","4 m/s","20 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The total momentum of the rifle and bullet is zero before and after firing. So $M V = m v$, which gives $V = \\frac{0.02 \\times 400}{4} = 2$ m/s. A common trap is forgetting to convert 20 g to 0.02 kg.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":172,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a bullet is fired from a gun that is initially at rest, the total momentum of the (bullet + gun) system just after firing is zero.\nReason (R): The force on the bullet by the gun and the force on the gun by the bullet are equal and opposite and act for the same time interval, so the momentum changes they produce cancel.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the third law, $\\vec F$ and $-\\vec F$ act for a common time $\\Delta t$. So the changes in momentum, $\\vec F\\Delta t$ and $-\\vec F\\Delta t$, add to zero, and the total momentum stays at its initial value of zero. R is exactly the reasoning that leads to A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":173,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Two blocks of masses 3 kg and 2 kg are placed in contact on a smooth horizontal floor. A horizontal force of 20 N is applied to the 3 kg block, pushing both blocks together. What is the magnitude of the contact force between the two blocks?","image":"","options":["8 N","12 N","20 N","4 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Both blocks move together with $a = \\frac{20}{3+2} = 4$ m/s$^2$. The only horizontal force on the 2 kg block is the contact force, so it equals $2 \\times 4 = 8$ N. A value of 12 N would be the net force on the 3 kg block, not the contact force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":174,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A uniform rope of mass 4 kg and length 2 m lies on a smooth horizontal floor. It is pulled along its length by a horizontal force of 12 N applied at one end. What is the tension at the midpoint of the rope?","image":"","options":["12 N","6 N","3 N","0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The whole rope accelerates at $\\frac{12}{4} = 3$ m/s$^2$. The rear half has mass 2 kg, and the only horizontal force on it is the tension at the midpoint, so $T = 2 \\times 3 = 6$ N. Tension is constant along a string only if the string is massless.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":175,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a person standing in a lift accelerating upward, the normal force from the floor exceeds the person's weight. This shows that Newton's third law fails in accelerated situations.\nStatement II: The weight of the person and the normal force on the person by the floor are not an action-reaction pair, so they need not be equal.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Weight and normal force both act on the person, so their inequality says nothing about the third law. The normal force on the person by the floor is always equal and opposite to the force on the floor by the person. The equality mg = R holds only in equilibrium and has no connection with the third law.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":176,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"In an Atwood machine, masses m and 3m hang from a light inextensible string passing over a light, frictionless pulley. Match the quantities in List-I with their values in List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Magnitude of acceleration of each mass\n(Q) Tension in the string\n(R) Net force on the lighter mass\n(S) Total force exerted by the string on the pulley\nList-II\n(1) $3mg$\n(2) $\\frac{mg}{2}$\n(3) $\\frac{g}{2}$\n(4) $\\frac{3mg}{2}$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4","P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The acceleration is $a = \\frac{2m}{4m}g = \\frac{g}{2}$. The tension is $T = \\frac{2(m)(3m)g}{4m} = \\frac{3mg}{2}$, and the net force on the lighter mass is $ma = \\frac{mg}{2}$. Both string segments pull down on the pulley, giving $2T = 3mg$.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":177,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stone of mass 0.25 kg tied to the end of a string is whirled round in a circle of radius 1.5 m with a speed of 40 rev./min in a horizontal plane. What is the tension in the string?","image":"","options":["2.5 N","6.6 N","0.17 N","9.9 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The angular speed is ω = 40 × 2π/60 ≈ 4.19 rad s$^{-1}$. The tension provides the centripetal force, so T = mω²r = 0.25 × 17.5 × 1.5 ≈ 6.6 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.21, converted to MCQ)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":178,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block lies on a plane whose inclination is slowly increased. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the plane is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$. At what angle with the horizontal does the block just begin to slide?","image":"","options":["$60^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$30^\\circ$","$15^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When sliding just begins, $\\tan\\theta_{max} = \\mu_s = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$, which gives $\\theta_{max} = 30^\\circ$. This angle does not depend on the mass of the block.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-179","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":179,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a gun fires a bullet, the kinetic energy acquired by the gun is much smaller than that acquired by the bullet.\nReason (R): The gun and bullet acquire momenta of equal magnitude, and for a given momentum the kinetic energy $\\frac{p^2}{2m}$ is inversely proportional to mass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Momentum conservation makes $|p_g| = |p_b|$. Since $K = \\frac{p^2}{2m}$, the far heavier gun gets much less kinetic energy. R therefore directly explains A.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-180","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":180,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The momentum of a particle moving along a straight line varies with time as $p = 2t^2 + 3t$ (p in kg m/s, t in s). What is the magnitude of the force on the particle at $t = 2$ s?","image":"","options":["11 N","14 N","8 N","7 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the second law, $F = \\frac{dp}{dt} = 4t + 3$, which gives 11 N at $t = 2$ s. 14 N is the momentum at that instant, not the force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-181","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":181,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (body in uniform circular motion) with List-II (force that provides the centripetal force).\nList-I:\n(A) Stone whirled in a horizontal circle by a string\n(B) Planet revolving around the sun\n(C) Car turning on a level road\n(D) Car on a banked road moving at the optimum speed\nList-II:\n(I) Static friction between tyres and road\n(II) Horizontal component of the normal reaction\n(III) Tension in the string\n(IV) Gravitational force due to the sun","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Centripetal force is not a new kind of force. In each case a real force plays the role: tension for the stone, the sun's gravity for the planet, and static friction on the level road. At the optimum speed on a banked road, friction is not needed, so the horizontal component of $N$ alone provides it.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-182","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":182,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A curved road of radius 120 m is banked at an angle $\\theta$ with $\\tan\\theta = 0.75$. At what speed can a car take this turn so that no friction is needed at all? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["24 m/s","30 m/s","36 m/s","40 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The optimum speed is $v_o = \\sqrt{R g \\tan\\theta} = \\sqrt{120 \\times 10 \\times 0.75} = 30$ m/s. At this speed the horizontal component of the normal reaction alone supplies the centripetal force.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-183","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":183,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block of mass 5 kg rests on a rough horizontal floor. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the floor is 0.5. A horizontal force of 10 N is applied to the block. What is the magnitude of the frictional force acting on the block? (Take g = 10 m/s^2)","image":"","options":["10 N","25 N","0 N","15 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The limiting friction is $\\mu_s mg = 0.5 \\times 50 = 25$ N. The applied force of 10 N is less than this, so the block stays at rest. Static friction adjusts itself to exactly balance the applied force, so it is 10 N, not 25 N.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-184","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":184,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A particle is in equilibrium under three concurrent forces. Two of them are 3 N along the x-axis and 4 N along the y-axis. What is the magnitude of the third force?","image":"","options":["5 N","7 N","1 N","12 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In equilibrium the third force must be equal and opposite to the resultant of the other two. That resultant is $\\sqrt{3^2+4^2} = 5$ N. Simply adding the magnitudes (7 N) ignores that the two forces are perpendicular vectors.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-185","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":185,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A stone is gently released from the window of a train moving on a straight horizontal track with an acceleration of $2\\ m/s^2$. Neglect air resistance.\nAssertion (A): Just after release, the stone has no horizontal acceleration.\nReason (R): Just after release, the horizontal velocity of the stone with respect to the ground is zero.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The second law is local: once the stone leaves the train, the only force on it is gravity, so its horizontal acceleration is zero. However, it keeps the horizontal velocity the train had at that instant, so R is false. Zero horizontal acceleration comes from zero horizontal force, not from zero horizontal velocity.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-186","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":186,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"In Galileo's double inclined plane experiment, a ball is released from rest on one smooth plane and rolls up the second plane. In the ideal frictionless limit, the slope of the second plane is reduced to zero, so it becomes horizontal. What happens to the ball?","image":"","options":["It stops after travelling a distance equal to the length of the first plane","It keeps moving forever with constant velocity","It keeps accelerating on the horizontal plane","It slows down uniformly and stops when its kinetic energy is used up"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"With no friction, the ball would try to regain its initial height, which it can never do on a horizontal plane, so its motion never ceases. This led Galileo to the law of inertia.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-187","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":187,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A plank on a smooth floor is pulled forward, and a block resting on it moves along with the plank without slipping. The friction force on the block by the plank acts in the forward direction.\nReason (R): Frictional force always opposes the motion of a body.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Static friction is the only horizontal force on the block, and it accelerates the block forward along with the plank. Friction opposes relative motion (impending or actual) between surfaces, not motion itself, so R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-188","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":188,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"The position of a particle of mass 0.5 kg is given by $\\vec{r} = (t^3\\,\\hat{i} + 2t^2\\,\\hat{j})$ m, where t is in seconds. What is the magnitude of the force acting on it at t = 2 s?","image":"","options":["6.32 N","12.65 N","7.21 N","4.0 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Differentiating twice gives $\\vec{a} = 6t\\,\\hat{i} + 4\\,\\hat{j}$, which equals $(12\\hat{i} + 4\\hat{j})$ m/s$^2$ at t = 2 s. Then $\\vec{F} = (6\\hat{i} + 2\\hat{j})$ N, with magnitude $\\sqrt{40} \\approx 6.32$ N. The value 7.21 N comes from wrongly using the velocity instead of the acceleration.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-189","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":189,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"A block hangs at rest from a horizontal ceiling, supported by two light strings attached on either side. One string makes $30^\\circ$ with the ceiling and the other makes $60^\\circ$ with the ceiling. Each string can withstand a maximum tension of 100 N. What is the maximum mass of the block that can be supported? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["11.5 kg","20.0 kg","10.0 kg","17.3 kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The horizontal balance gives $T_1\\cos 30^\\circ = T_2\\cos 60^\\circ$, so $T_2 = \\sqrt{3}T_1$. The vertical balance then gives $T_1 = \\frac{mg}{2}$ and $T_2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}mg$. The steeper string carries the larger tension, so it reaches 100 N first: $mg = \\frac{200}{\\sqrt{3}}$ N, giving $m \\approx 11.5$ kg. The answer 20 kg wrongly applies the limit to the less-stressed string.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-190","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":190,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Galileo's double inclined plane experiment with smooth (but not ideal) planes, the ball rises on the second plane to a height slightly less than, but never greater than, its initial height.\nStatement II: If the slope of the second plane is decreased, the ball rises to a smaller height but travels the same distance along the plane.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Real friction makes the final height slightly less than the initial height, never more. On a gentler slope the ball still reaches nearly the same height, but it travels a longer distance, so Statement II is wrong.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-191","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":191,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"One end of a string of length l is connected to a particle of mass m and the other to a small peg on a smooth horizontal table. If the particle moves in a circle with speed v the net force on the particle (directed towards the centre) is: (T is the tension in the string.)","image":"","options":["$T$","$T - \\frac{mv^2}{l}$","$T + \\frac{mv^2}{l}$","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"On a smooth horizontal table, the only horizontal force on the particle is the tension, which points towards the peg. Tension alone provides the centripetal force, so the net force is T (which equals $mv^2/l$).","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 (Exercise 4.4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-192","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":192,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A seasoned cricketer pulls his hands backward while catching a fast-moving ball, so the ball stings his hands less.\nReason (R): Pulling the hands backward reduces the change in momentum of the ball during the catch.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The ball's momentum changes from mv to zero whether or not the hands move back, so the change in momentum is the same. Moving the hands back lengthens the stopping time, which lowers the average force $\\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}$. So A is true and R is false.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-LAWS-OF-MOTION-193","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Laws of Motion","qno":193,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Laws of Motion","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the same force acts for the same time on a light body and a heavy body, both initially at rest, the two bodies acquire the same speed.\nReason (R): The same force acting for the same time produces the same change in momentum in different bodies.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Both bodies gain equal momentum $F\\Delta t$. Since $v = p/m$, the lighter body picks up a greater speed, so A is false while R is true.","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 4 · Laws of Motion","difficulty":"easy"}]});
